   "source_pool_hash": "e8462d39af704c8eddafda63960308e04102442fab84260751abec50c54c22e5",
   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThe Carbon Trust is a consultancy, and this item reads its public pages: a maritime page, the page for its Route to Net Zero Standard, its front page and two records about the organisation. The one quantified research statement is on shipping: «While shipping goods can be 17 times more efficient than air, and 10 times more efficient than road, figures suggest that the sector represents almost 3% of global CO2 emissions. 1 There have also been projections that maritime shipping could account for 17% of total annual CO2 emissions by 2050, if left unchecked.»\n\nThe same page lists work it has done on fuels: «our in-depth Commercial Readiness of Alternative Shipping Fuels Study assessed the merits and drawbacks of five alternative shipping fuels across each supply chain stage, with a focus on the European context.» It also describes a cost-sharing scheme: «The Blue Visby Solution combines technology with long-established maritime traditions of cost-sharing and collaboration.»\n\nThe front page reports a survey and a technology figure: «We asked 400 businesses around the world what is holding them back on Net Zero and provided solutions to address these barriers by making the internal business case and tackling Scope 3 emissions.» «Nearly 60% of the technologies crucial for reaching Net Zero by 2050 haven’t achieved commercial scale.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the 17 times and 10 times efficiency comparison and the 3% and 17% emission shares come from a service page that cites one footnote, so they are the Carbon Trust's summary of other work and not its own study. Methodology and limitations are therefore thin: the footnote reads «Fourth Greenhouse Gas Study 2020 (imo.org)» and the page states no sample, model or date for the 2050 projection beyond that footnote.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\n*Analytical inference:* the shipping comparison matters because it sets shipping against air and road per unit of cargo while the page still puts the sector at almost 3% of global CO2 emissions, so a customer's emissions question to a forwarder will often be about ocean legs. The page gives no emissions factor and no cost per tonne, so it cannot be used to calculate a shipment's emissions.\n\nThe Route to Net Zero Standard is a certification scheme the Carbon Trust runs. It states how it works: «We have developed 3 certification tiers to guide you on your journey to Net Zero.» «As you progress through the levels, the qualifying requirements become more challenging and cover more aspects of carbon management.» It lists what each level covers: «Taking Action Historical reductions in operational emissions GHG emissions reduction target Foundational CO 2 e management practices Advancing Science-aligned reductions in emissions Science-aligned reduction target Advancing CO 2 e management practices Leading 1.5C aligned reductions in emissions Net Zero target» It adds that «we will independently verify your carbon reductions»\n\n*Analytical inference:* a supplier or customer holding one of these certificates would have its emissions reductions checked by the Carbon Trust, so the holder's reductions have been checked by the Carbon Trust. The page does not say whether any freight company holds a certificate or how many organisations do.\n\nOn border carbon cost, the front page records commentary: «The EU launched the world’s first carbon border tariff with the Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism.» The held pages give no tariff rate and no freight effect.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\n*Analytical inference:* on claims, none of the held pages supports a freight emissions claim for a specific lane or shipment. What they support is a general statement that shipping is efficient per unit moved and large in total, attributed to the Carbon Trust's summary of a study cited in the page's footnote.\n\nOn decisions, the scheme states «We have developed 3 certification tiers to guide you on your journey to Net Zero.» and so gives a way to describe a client's position: the scheme's own scope list runs from a reduction target to a net zero target. A workspace that asks suppliers for emissions evidence can ask which level, if any, they hold. The Carbon Trust also states its view that decarbonization needs technology that is not yet at scale: «Nearly 60% of the technologies crucial for reaching Net Zero by 2050 haven’t achieved commercial scale.»\n\nOn the Carbon Trust's own commitments, it states: «Reduce our Scope 1 and 2 emissions by 50% by 2030 from a 2018 base year» «Engage with suppliers to ensure that by 2026 at least 56% of our suppliers by emissions are covered by a science-based target» «Reach Net Zero and reduce our absolute emissions by 90% by 2050 from a 2018 base year» It adds that its net zero goal is «validated by the Science Based Targets initiative (SBTi)»\n\n*Analytical inference:* these are the organisation's targets for itself, and not requirements on its clients or on forwarders. The 2026 supplier target year is the current year and the pages give no result against it.\n\n## 4. Client Conversation Talking Points and Public Position\n\n*Analytical inference:* what the workspace can say is that a UK consultancy offers a verified, tiered route to net zero and has published work on shipping fuels. A useful question for a client is whether it needs third-party verification of supplier emissions data and which scheme it accepts.\n\n*Analytical inference:* pitfalls are to present the Carbon Trust's maritime percentages as its own research, to cite its commercial services as an independent standard, or to describe the commercial readiness study's conclusions, which are not in the held pages. A caution on the encyclopedia page held here: «This article needs additional citations for verification»\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nThe held pages do not give the results of the fuel study or the survey of 400 businesses, so what they conclude is open. The fuel study is described only as: «our in-depth Commercial Readiness of Alternative Shipping Fuels Study assessed the merits and drawbacks of five alternative shipping fuels across each supply chain stage, with a focus on the European context.» The shipping figures rest on one footnoted source and no date is stated for the 2050 projection other than the year itself.\n\nThe organisation's evidence-agreement language is thin. The maritime sentence describes the 2050 figure as a projection: «While shipping goods can be 17 times more efficient than air, and 10 times more efficient than road, figures suggest that the sector represents almost 3% of global CO2 emissions. 1 There have also been projections that maritime shipping could account for 17% of total annual CO2 emissions by 2050, if left unchecked.» The page gives no peer review or independent confirmation.\n\nOn who published or issued the finding, the encyclopedia states: «The Carbon Trust is a consultancy established in March 2001, with offices across the world.» The company register adds: «Incorporated on 29 March 2001» «Nature of business (SIC) 74901 - Environmental consulting activities» The register page itself warns: «Companies House does not check the accuracy of the information filed» The Carbon Trust describes its independence this way: «We are financially independent and invest our profit back into enhancing the breadth and reach of our work.» That is a self-description.\n\n- 2026: Carbon Trust target year for 56% of its suppliers by emissions to be covered by a science-based target\n- 2030: Carbon Trust target year for a 50% cut in its Scope 1 and 2 emissions from 2018\n- 2050: Carbon Trust net zero target year, with a 90% absolute cut from 2018\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- www.carbontrust.com: https://www.carbontrust.com/what-we-do/market-transformation/maritime-decarbonisation\n- www.carbontrust.com: https://www.carbontrust.com/what-we-do/net-zero-transition-planning-and-delivery/route-to-net-zero-standard\n- publications.carbontrust.com: https://publications.carbontrust.com/\n- find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk: https://find-and-update.company-information.service.gov.uk/company/04190230\n- en.wikipedia.org: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carbon_Trust",
