   "item_id": "45dab7a6-ac61-48b2-a9af-24be8568ba02",
   "source_pool_hash": "eca941a9c1e85b697c8af4a15c9707acb6d092108113a2413114bb760abea284",
   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThe source is a research centre's project listing, not a single study. It is the Cranfield School of Management's Centre for Logistics, Procurement and Supply Chain Management, which describes its work this way: «Our research interests are in the areas of agile and lean supply chains, procurement, interorganisational relationships, supply chain strategy, decision support systems, information systems, sustainability and circular economy, food and retail supply chains, simulation and modelling, and manufacturing supply chain.»\n\nThe one project with a stated outcome is Project SCALE, a food and drink logistics project across North-West Europe. The centre says «We identified three key building blocks which are needed for businesses to make a meaningful step change in their approach to supply chain sustainability». The three are «belief that improving the TBL can really improve competitiveness and profitability; recognition that progress in this area is not easy and that certain core enablers must be in place to drive real change; and articulation of a compelling TBL value proposition with an action plan for implementation.» TBL is the page's abbreviation for the Triple Bottom Line.\n\nThe methodology limits are those of a case-study page. The page says «The project involved a wide range of approaches including case studies, workshops, desk-based research, surveys, modelling and pilots.» It gives no sample size, effect size or measured result for the three building blocks. The scope is food and drink supply chain logistics: «Project SCALE (Step Change in Agri-Food Ecosystems) aimed to increase economic competitiveness but, as importantly, to also improve environmental and social sustainability of food and drink supply chain logistics across North-West Europe.»\n\nThe other projects on the page are described by their aims, such as «This project will establish the UK national clean Maritime Research (UK-MaRes) hub to accelerate the decarbonisation and elimination of harmful air pollution for the maritime sector.» and «This project will develop a novel Events Environmental Sustainability Platform to help events management companies and their clients verify their Greenhouse Gas emissions, and to make recommendations on how to reduce event related GHG emissions.» Both are stated as aims, so no result of either is held.\n\n*Analytical inference:* the SCALE building blocks are the centre's account of its own project; the page does not say they were tested outside it.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\n*Analytical inference:* the page matters to a forwarder as a map of who is researching freight-relevant decarbonisation in the UK, not as a source of a cost or rate. Three entries bear on freight or its clients. The maritime hub names ports and carriers among its partners: «the Port of Felixstowe, the Port of Dover, Peel Ports, Cosco Shipping (UK), DFDS, MOL Group, Freeport East, Rux Energy, and many more.» The listing includes a project on «freight distribution in urban areas», described as «The project will contribute to our understanding of freight distribution in urban areas.» And the events platform is aimed at emissions verification for event organisers and their clients.\n\nThe funding scale puts the hub in context. The page gives the hub's dates, sponsors and Cranfield's share as «Dates 1st September 2023 to 31st March 2027 Sponsor EPSRC and Department for Transport Funded £345,496 out of» the total value of the project. The same page links a news article about a national research hub with a different, larger sum in its title, and the page does not reconcile that sum with the project total.\n\n*Operational implication:* a forwarder with maritime or event clients in the UK can use the partner list to see which ports and shipping lines are in the hub. The page states no cost, surcharge or rule from any project.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\nWhat it changes is the wording of claims, not an operating decision. The decision-relevant statement is the SCALE one, that the building blocks are «needed for businesses to make a meaningful step change in their approach to supply chain sustainability». The workspace can say that Cranfield reports these three building blocks from its SCALE project; it cannot say the page proves that sustainability improves competitiveness, because the first building block is a belief the centre lists, not a measured result.\n\nThe events platform is described in the future tense: «This project will develop a novel Events Environmental Sustainability Platform». A claim that such a platform exists, or that it verifies an event's emissions, is not supported by the page. The UK National Hydrogen Strategy entry says Cranfield «concluded that decarbonising the nation will require a massive increase in electricity supply», which is the centre's stated conclusion about national electricity demand.\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those statements, the page supports saying that named UK research projects on maritime decarbonisation, urban freight and event emissions are under way; it does not support a quantified benefit to any client.\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nWhat is not resolved is every quantified outcome. The page gives no result for the UK-MaRes hub, whose dates run to 31st March 2027, and none for the events platform, which the page states only as «This project will develop a novel Events Environmental Sustainability Platform». It also does not reconcile the project total value with the sum in the linked article title, described in section 2.\n\nOn how settled the work is, the page gives no language about peer review, independent confirmation or dispute of the SCALE building blocks, so the evidence-agreement signal is not stated. It does say the project «led to a number of academic articles and publications», without naming any.\n\nOn who issued it, the SCALE page says «We were the lead partner in the three-year project which involved other research institutions (University of Artois and Wageningen University), working in collaboration with DHL and European Food and Farming Partnerships (EFFP).» The hub page names «Principal Investigator: Professor Ying Xie» and says it is «Led by Durham University and in collaboration with 12 other universities».\n\n- 1st September 2023: start of the UK-MaRes hub project, as the page lists «1st September 2023 to 31st March 2027» (past)\n- 31st March 2027: end date of the UK-MaRes hub project, stated as the end of the listed project dates\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- www.cranfield.ac.uk: https://www.cranfield.ac.uk/som/research-centres/centre-for-logistics-procurement-and-supply-chain-management\n- www.cranfield.ac.uk: https://www.cranfield.ac.uk/som/research-centres/centre-for-logistics-procurement-and-supply-chain-management/research-projects-current-and-past\n- www.cranfield.ac.uk: https://www.cranfield.ac.uk/som/research-projects/uk-national-clean-maritime-research-hub\n- www.cranfield.ac.uk: https://www.cranfield.ac.uk/som/case-studies/project-scale\n",
