   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThis item is a review notice for a report that is still in draft, so it does not yet hold a scientific finding. The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) states the status of the draft: «The SOD of the IPCC Special Report on Climate Change and Cities is available for Expert Review from 8 May to 3 July 2026.»\n\nThe notice says what the report is for: «The report aims to provide a timely assessment of the latest science related to climate change and cities, including climate impacts and risks, as well as adaptation and mitigation options.» The agreed outline, as the IPCC's 2024 announcement describes it, covers: «The agreed outline of the Special Report on Climate Change and Cities includes trends, challenges and opportunities for cities in a changing climate, the actions and solutions to reduce urban risks and emissions, how to facilitate and accelerate change in the context of cities, and solutions by city and types and regions.»\n\nThe draft has already been through one round of review: «During the Expert Review from October to December 2025, the First-Order Draft of the Special Report on Climate Change and Cities received more than 32,000 comments from 1,365 registered Expert Reviewers around the world» The notice places the current round in the process: «The review of the Second-Order Draft is the next step in the IPCC’s rigorous multiple-stage review process.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the draft's conclusions are not held on any page, so this summary describes the report's scope and review stage, and any statement of what the report finds would be unsupported.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\nThe IPCC says the second draft adds a summary for decision makers: «This Second-Order Draft will also include the first draft of the Summary for Policymakers (SPM), a key product that encapsulates the report’s findings and is aimed at informing policymakers shaping climate action worldwide.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the held pages describe the report's subject as climate change and cities and name no freight, delivery or logistics topic in the outline. The scoping meeting's expertise list does name transport and supply chain among the areas covered: «Impacts and Risks, including (i) Economic and Non-economic Losses and Damages and (ii) Compounding and Cascading Aspects (expertise examples include statistical climatology, detection and attribution of climate extremes, heat islands and urban overheating, air pollution, inland and coastal flooding, critical infrastructure including power, digital communication and transport, water/energy/food/health nexus, food security, health, supply chain, vulnerability, losses and damages, risk management, disaster risk reduction, risk modelling);»\n\n*Analytical inference:* because that list names transport and supply chain only as expertise areas of the people who scoped the report, it does not show that the final report will assess urban freight. What can be said is that the report is the IPCC's assessment of cities, and a city-level climate rule or a municipal client's procurement question may later cite it. The pages do not say that any such rule or procurement requirement exists.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\n*Analytical inference:* on claims, a draft under review should not be cited as an IPCC finding. The IPCC describes the steps still to come: «Following receipt of the review comments, author teams will prepare final drafts of the full report and SPM, taking into account review comments received.» Then: «The final draft of the report will be distributed to governments for a final round of written comments on the SPM, before governments meet in plenary session to approve the SPM line by line and accept the underlying report.»\n\nOn timing, the notice states: «Scheduled for release in March 2027, the Special Report on Climate Change and Cities will be the first IPCC report published in the seventh assessment cycle.» It adds: «It is also the only special report in the current cycle.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the decision this changes is when a city-related sustainability claim can lean on the report. Until the plenary accepts the report, the safe position is to cite earlier IPCC assessments or to say the special report is in preparation. The notice gives no date earlier than the scheduled release at which the summary becomes citable.\n\n## 4. Client Conversation Talking Points and Public Position\n\n*Analytical inference:* what the workspace can credibly say is that the IPCC is preparing its first report of the seventh assessment cycle and that it is a special report on cities. A question to put to a client with city-based customers is whether any of its contracts or tenders refer to IPCC material and which edition they cite.\n\n*Analytical inference:* pitfalls are to describe draft text as final, to attribute freight conclusions to a report whose outline does not name freight, or to assume the review window is still open. The IPCC states the review is part of a controlled process: «IPCC reports are drafted and reviewed in several stages, thus guaranteeing objectivity and transparency.»\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nThe window the notice stated was 8 May to 3 July 2026, with registration for expert reviewers stated as: «The registration of experts closes on 26 June 2026.» Both dates have passed, and no held page reports how many further comments the second round drew. What the second draft says is also not held, and the IPCC states when the record becomes public: «The comments and the authors’ responses, together with the drafts, are published after the report is finalized.»\n\nThe notice does not say whether the report assesses freight emissions, and the held pages give no evidence that it will; that stays open until the final report is available. Whether the draft's scientific conclusions are settled is also open: the only agreement language held is about process, that the reviews exist, not about any result.\n\nThe notice names the body issuing it: «The Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) is the UN body for assessing the science related to climate change.»\n\n- 8 May to 3 July 2026: Second-Order Draft expert review window\n- 26 June 2026: expert reviewer registration closes\n- 3 July 2026: expert review closes\n- March 2027: scheduled release of the Special Report on Climate Change and Cities\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- www.ipcc.ch: https://www.ipcc.ch/2026/05/08/ipcc-srcities-second-order-draft-review/\n- www.ipcc.ch: https://www.ipcc.ch/report/special-report-on-climate-change-and-cities/\n- www.ipcc.ch: https://www.ipcc.ch/2024/08/02/ipcc-approves-outlines-of-the-first-two-reports-in-the-seventh-assessment-cycle/",
