   "body": "## 1. What's Being Tested or Deployed and By Whom\n\nOperators with a quantified result are few in the sources, and the main account comes from NACFE, a trucking efficiency body, in its recap of 2024. It reports that «Kodiak expanded its over-the-road trucking operations, completing 900 autonomous deliveries for Martin Brower.» and that «Pony.ai held its initial public offering on Nasdaq, raising $260 million. The company operates a fleet of more than 250 robotaxis and 190 autonomous trucks, with more than 2.4 million miles driven autonomously.» On the yard side, «Autonomous truck startup Outrider secured $62 million in Series D funding to scale its self-driving truck services in distribution yards.» and on the road, «Aurora announced a long-term partnership with NVIDIA and Continental to develop and deploy self-driving trucks.» NACFE adds that «Aurora plans to launch an autonomous trucking service in Texas by April of this year.» The recap is a year old and the sources do not report whether that launch took place. The exits are as visible as the entries: «In March, Embark laid off approximately 70% of its workforce and closed its Southern California and Houston offices.» and «TuSimple announced in December 2023 that it would discontinue its U.S. operations and redirect its focus to the Asia-Pacific market. By December 2024, the company rebranded as CreateAI and transitioned to AI gaming technology.» NACFE also says «In July 2023, Waymo suspended its autonomous trucking division, Waymo Via, to prioritize its robotaxi services». On the connected side, NACFE reports «In May, Los Angeles partnered with a leading V2X provider to install smart traffic lights along key freight corridors, optimizing traffic flow and reducing idling times, thereby reducing fuel consumption by up to 15%.» This is NACFE's own account of a city partnership, not a measured result from the city. The NACFE recap also gives company-level savings and corridor cost figures for large shippers that no other source in this item confirms, so they are not repeated here. The International Transport Forum (ITF) adds the policy view in a report with «Date of Publication 31 May 2017», writing that «Today’s technology already makes it possible to operate automated trucks.» *Analytical inference:* the sources show a field of several trials and a number of exits, with the largest named fleet (190 autonomous trucks) run by a company whose robotaxi fleet is larger still; the sources give no lane-level service result beyond the delivery count NACFE reports for Kodiak.\n\n## 2. What This Tells Us About Industry Trajectory\n\nThe ITF report, published in 2017, stated its position on the stakes as follows: «Reduced reliance on humans to move road freight in the future could offer large cost savings for businesses and consumers. It could also disrupt the careers and lives of millions of professional truck drivers.» Its recommendation on pace was to «Consider a temporary permit system to manage the speed of adoption and to support a just transition for displaced drivers, while ensuring fair access to markets.» The ITF notes that three industry bodies took part: «The International Road Transport Unions, the International Transport Workers’ Federation and the European Automobile Manufacturers Association contributed data and insights on driverless technology in the road freight sector as well as funds for the research.» Rule-making has followed in steps. NACFE reports that «The legal framework for autonomous trucking in the United States remained fragmented, presenting substantial challenges for nationwide deployment.» and that «The Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration introduced draft guidelines for autonomous trucking operations on interstate highways intended to harmonize state regulations. However, their adoption faced opposition from state governments and labor unions» and adds that «Disputes over liability in autonomous trucking accidents continued to pose legal uncertainties». The largest single step in these sources arrived in California: the Department of Motor Vehicles (DMV) release dated «FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE April 28, 2026» says «Under the updated regulations, AV manufacturers may now apply for permits to test and deploy heavy-duty autonomous vehicle technology on California roadways.» *Analytical inference:* the drivers in the sources are technology progress and state permitting, while labour and liability disputes slow it; the sources do not say when driverless trucks become routine for major freight contracts.\n\n## 3. Supplier Access and Procurement Reality\n\nThe sources describe makers and permit holders, not a market where a forwarder buys driverless capacity. On the vehicle side, NACFE reports that «Aurora announced a long-term partnership with NVIDIA and Continental to develop and deploy self-driving trucks.» and that «In April, delays at a major semiconductor factory in Taiwan had a cascading effect, postponing the delivery of new fleets by several months.» On permits, the California rule is aimed at makers: «Under the updated regulations, AV manufacturers may now apply for permits to test and deploy heavy-duty autonomous vehicle technology on California roadways.» Before deployment, «Manufacturers must complete 50,000 miles (light-duty) or 500,000 miles (heavy-duty) of testing at each phase» at each phase, which the DMV lists under permitting criteria. *Analytical inference:* the chain from a manufacturer's permit to a haulier's service offer has stages (safety-driver testing, driverless testing, commercial deployment) that the sources list in order, and a buyer's access depends on a maker passing them. *Legal Confirmation Required:* which party the 50,000 and 500,000 mile testing duties fall on for a given permit, a question for counsel. Gap: the sources name no price, lease or per-mile rate, no exclusive contract and no delivery date for driverless trucks, and none states how a haulier or forwarder buys capacity.\n\n## 4. Operational Fit by Transport Mode and Cargo Vertical\n\nRoad is the only mode the sources reach. California's release says it «Removes the prohibition on operating AVs with a Gross Vehicle Weight Rating (GVWR) of 10,001 pounds or more, opening the California market to AV freight operations.» The same release adds that «Heavy-duty AVs must still stop at CHP weigh stations and comply with all applicable state and federal commercial motor vehicle requirements.» and that it «Authorizes the DMV to impose targeted operational restrictions on AV manufacturers — including fleet size, location, speed and weather limits — when necessary for public safety.» The release also cites rules about «enabling law enforcement agencies to cite AV companies for moving violations committed by their vehicles». NACFE reports yard automation, where «Autonomous truck startup Outrider secured $62 million in Series D funding to scale its self-driving truck services in distribution yards.» The UNECE working party on automated and connected vehicles is described this way: «GRVA is the Working Party preparing draft regulations, guidance documents and interpretation documents for adoption by the parent body, WP.29.» with a remit to «GRVA deals with safety provisions related to the dynamics of vehicles (braking, steering), Advanced Driver Assistance Systems , Automated Driving Systems and well as cybersecurity provisions.» *Analytical inference:* the DMV items above are conditions on manufacturers and heavy-duty AVs in California; the sources do not say how they change which loads a driverless truck can take, and state no cargo vertical, payload or lane. *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether the DMV testing and weigh-station duties fall on a given manufacturer, haulier or lane, a question for counsel. Gap: the sources give no verdict for air or ocean and no data on temperature-controlled, high-value or hazardous freight.\n\n## 6. Conversational and Strategic Talking Points\n\n- Say that California has opened a permit route: «Under the updated regulations, AV manufacturers may now apply for permits to test and deploy heavy-duty autonomous vehicle technology on California roadways.»\n- Ask a haulage partner whether any driverless pilot it runs sits under a permit, since the same release says «Certain provisions are in immediate effect, while others will be phased in over the coming months, pursuant to effective dates.»\n- Avoid presenting driverless trucking as a settled service: NACFE reports exits and a fragmented United States legal framework alongside the launches.\n\n## 7. Time-to-Market, Procurement Window, and Action\n\nMONITORING: the sources give no date for driverless trucking capacity to be bought at scale; the dated points are the DMV release marked «FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE April 28, 2026» and the phased effective dates the DMV points to in its regulatory documents, which this item does not hold.\n\n- A road procurement lead, at the next California haulage tender, adds a question asking each haulier whether it has or plans a driverless pilot with a permit-holding manufacturer, and files the answer beside the DMV release of April 28, 2026. *Operational implication:* this separates a haulier's marketing language from a permitted pilot.\n- A compliance lead writes into the carrier vetting file the three DMV stages (safety-driver testing, driverless testing, commercial deployment) so that a claim of driverless capacity is checked against the stage actually reached.\n- A client services lead holds the client position at \"permits open for application, no service rate stated\" until a source gives a price or a start date.\n\n- 31 May 2017: ITF publishes its report on managing the transition to driverless road freight transport.\n- December 2024: by this date TuSimple had rebranded as CreateAI, per NACFE.\n- April 28, 2026: California DMV adopts new autonomous vehicle regulations covering heavy-duty vehicles.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- International Transport Forum, Managing the Transition to Driverless Road Freight Transport: https://itf-oecd.org/managing-transition-driverless-road-freight-transport\n- UNECE, Working Party on Automated/Autonomous and Connected Vehicles, Introduction: https://unece.org/transport/road-transport/working-party-automatedautonomous-and-connected-vehicles-introduction\n- California Department of Motor Vehicles, New Autonomous Vehicle Regulations Strengthen Oversight and Enforcement, Authorize Trucks and Transit: https://www.dmv.ca.gov/portal/news-and-media/new-autonomous-vehicle-regulations-strengthen-oversight-and-enforcement-authorize-trucks-and-transit/\n- NACFE, The State of Autonomous Trucking in 2025: A Recap of 2024: https://nacfe.org/autonomous-trucking/the-state-of-autonomous-trucking-in-2025-a-recap-of-2024/\n",
