   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThis is a policy analysis from the Global Maritime Forum, written with authors from the UCL Energy Institute and UMAS, of what the International Maritime Organization's (IMO) revised greenhouse gas strategy means for shipping. Its headline finding is that the strategy's 2040 checkpoint implies a very large cut in how much greenhouse gas each ship emits per unit of work: «The strategy’s target of 70% (striving for 80%) reduction in absolute emissions would likely require the average ship to reduce its greenhouse gas (GHG) intensity by ~90% by 2040, when increased trade volumes are accounted for.»\n\nThe analysis puts the nearer test at 2030: «More immediately, the strategy aims for 5-10% of the energy used in shipping in 2030 to have zero or near-zero GHG emissions.» The IMO's own page gives the strategy as «net-zero GHG emissions “by or around, i.e., close to 2050”, with indicative checkpoints for reducing GHG emissions by 20% (striving for 30%) by 2030 and 70% (striving for 80%) by 2040.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the 90% figure is the authors' own projection from the strategy's absolute target and traffic growth, as the quoted condition shows; the IMO page states the targets and does not state the 90% figure.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\nShipowners and fuel suppliers are the parties the analysis addresses: the fuel standard «will regulate the GHG intensity of fuel by setting a mandate on the maximum intensity and reducing the limit in set intervals (e.g. one, three or five years).» The authors add that this «will send a clear and unambiguous message to shipowners and fuel suppliers that the uptake of near-zero and zero-emission fuels must rapidly increase between now and 2040.» *Analytical inference:* for a forwarder buying ocean capacity, this is the route by which the fuel decisions of the shipowners it books with are expected to change by 2040.\n\n*Operational implication:* a forwarder that books long-term ocean capacity is dealing with shipowners whose fuel choices the analysis expects to change, and should ask each carrier how it plans to meet the 2030 zero-emission energy aim. The sources do not state how any fuel standard or pricing cost would reach a forwarder's invoice, so no surcharge or rate effect is quantified here.\n\nThe IMO page states a nearer, already in force, step: «IMO's short-term GHG reduction measures ( EEXI, CII rating ), developed to improve ship energy efficiency and reduce carbon intensity by at least 40% compared to 2008 levels by 2030, entered into force on 1 January 2023.»\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\nThe decision relevance is mostly timing. The analysis expected the policy basket to be settled in 2025: «The adoption of mid-term measures in 2025, including a fuel standard and a GHG pricing mechanism, will be crucial.» It also expected «adoption of the measures which is expected in the autumn of 2025, with entry into force no earlier than 2027». The IMO page now records that this did not happen on that schedule: «In October 2025, IMO's Marine Environment Protection Committee (MEPC) adjourned discussions on the adoption of draft amendments to MARPOL Annex VI, including the IMO Net-Zero Framework.»\n\nFor client conversations and tenders, a claim that carbon costs are coming from a binding IMO fuel standard or a pricing mechanism is therefore not yet supported: the framework had not been adopted when the IMO page was captured. The analysis advises the wider chain: «The wider shipping value chain should prepare for both policy measures and their respective implications and opportunities.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* because the October 2025 adjournment postpones adoption, any entry into force the analysis forecast as no earlier than 2027 rests on a decision that the IMO page shows is still to be taken.\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nThe methodology limits are those of a policy analysis: the 90% figure is a projection «when increased trade volumes are accounted for», and the text does not set out a model. The authors themselves flag that the effect of pricing is uncertain: «the exact emissions reduction outcome of a pricing mechanism is more uncertain, as it will not put a cap on emissions but rather make it more costly to emit». They also note that «there is a lack of consensus on what “just and equitable” means for the IMO’s GHG policy.»\n\nThe evidence agreement signal in the text is the authors' statement that the strategy is «As an agreed UN Strategy, adopted unanimously» and removes uncertainty about the transition; that describes the policy, not the 90% projection, and the piece cites no contrary study; the IMO page is a statement of the strategy's targets and does not assess the authors' reading. The source authority signal is the byline: «Dr Tristan Smith Associate Professor, UCL Energy Institute; Director, UMAS», with the Global Maritime Forum as publisher on «1 December 2023», and the IMO page as the issuer of the strategy.\n\nThe IMO page lists «MEPC 84 (Spring 2026)», a meeting date that has now passed; the pages held do not state its outcome. It also lists «Target dates: MEPC 85 (Autumn 2026) MEPC 86 (Summer 2027) - Initiate the review of the 2023 IMO GHG Strategy», of which the Autumn 2026 meeting is also dated and its outcome is not held. No IRENA page is among the pages held, although the title of this item names IRENA.\n\n- 1 January 2023: IMO short-term measures (EEXI and CII rating) «entered into force on 1 January 2023»\n- July 2023: IMO Member States agreed the revised GHG strategy, «A key milestone was reached in July 2023»\n- April 2025: MEPC 83 finalized the draft legal text called the IMO Net-Zero Framework, «finalized a draft legal text for these measures, referred to as the \" IMO Net-Zero Framework \", in April 2025»\n- October 2025: MEPC adjourned discussion on adopting the draft amendments, «adjourned discussions on the adoption of draft amendments to MARPOL Annex VI»\n- 2030: indicative checkpoint, «5-10% of the energy used in shipping in 2030»\n- 2040: indicative checkpoint, «70% (striving for 80%) by 2040»\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- imo.org: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/cutting-ghg-emissions.aspx\n- globalmaritimeforum.org: https://globalmaritimeforum.org/insight/the-implications-of-the-imo-revised-ghg-strategy-for-shipping/",
