  {
   "item_id": "85525e8f-04ce-4e4d-9a9e-8c948d322eb9",
   "source_pool_hash": "0a61774711379ae8d110277ab6d3279ef2fb3a14ecbee97e69beca398abf1dd9",
   "body": "## 1. What's Being Tested or Deployed and By Whom\n\nThe International Energy Agency (IEA) reports in its Global EV Outlook 2025 that «the sector whose demand grew the most was electric trucks, growing over 75% in 2024 to reach nearly 3% of global EV battery demand.» Where it happened: «Electric truck battery demand was driven by growth in China, but demand also ramped up in Europe (about 25%), which accounted for about 10% of the global total.» For scale against all EV batteries, the same report states «In 2024, EV battery demand grew by over 30% in China». The sources name no individual trucking operator and no named truck fleet; the activity they describe is by market and by battery maker. CALSTART, a clean transportation industry body, wrote in a post dated «February 27, 2026» that the «deployment of LFP batteries surpassed nickel-based chemistries for the first time» in 2025, and that «battery manufacturer CATL, in partnership with Changan Automobile, unveiled the first passenger EV featuring a sodium-ion battery». *Analytical inference:* electric truck batteries are a small but fastest-growing slice of a battery market the IEA describes as driven overwhelmingly by cars, so truck buyers share a supply base with the car industry rather than having one of their own.\n\n## 2. What This Tells Us About Industry Trajectory\n\nThe IEA projects that «the contribution of electric trucks to EV battery demand triples by 2030 to reach more than 8%, up from nearly 3% in 2024.» In its report on battery supply chain sustainability it adds that battery demand overall is set to continue growing fast on current policy settings, «increasing four-and-a-half times by 2030 and more than seven times by 2035». On price, the IEA reports «Prices for lithium-ion battery packs fell 20% in 2024 – the largest drop since 2017 – as a result of low critical mineral prices and battery margins being squeezed through competition, predominantly in China.» CALSTART, citing BloombergNEF, puts the later level at «lithium-ion battery pack pricing had dropped 8% since then, costing $108 per kilowatt-hour.» The two price statements come from different publishers, so they are read as direction (down in 2024, down again since) and not as one series. The IEA also warns that the same low prices carry a risk: the mineral surplus «is expected to persist over the next few years, but low prices could discourage future investments and might cause supply shortages for lithium and nickel by 2030.» *Analytical inference:* the IEA demand figures show electric truck battery growth led by China, then Europe; the sources name no truck operator, regulation or client demand signal as the driver of truck uptake, and the pack price statements describe batteries, not truck purchases.\n\n## 3. Supplier Access and Procurement Reality\n\nThe sources describe battery supply, not truck sales. Pack price declines were uneven: «with the fastest declines seen in China, where prices fell nearly 30% in 2024, compared to 10-15% in Europe and the» United States, which the IEA says widened the gap between China and the rest of the world. The IEA reports that «lithium iron phosphate (LFP) batteries – the main battery chemistry used in China – being almost 30% cheaper per kilowatt-hour (kWh) than lithium nickel cobalt manganese oxide (NMC) batteries», and that NMC remains the most widely used chemistry in the United States and Europe. Carnegie Mellon University researchers find that «production at multiple stages of the supply chain across battery technologies occurs in China, primarily due to its role in materials refining and cathode production», and the university article adds: «China restricted exports of graphite, another key material used in batteries.» *Analytical inference:* a buyer outside China, choosing between LFP and NMC electric trucks, is choosing between a cheaper chemistry whose supply sits mainly in one country and a more energy dense chemistry with more materials to source from more places; the Carnegie Mellon finding is that those vulnerabilities differ by chemistry. Gap: no source names an electric truck maker, a delivery lead time, a purchase volume or an exclusive supply arrangement for electric trucks, so who can buy, at what scale and how fast is not stated.\n\n## 4. Operational Fit by Transport Mode and Cargo Vertical\n\nRoad is the only mode the sources reach. The IEA reports that «in China, electric truck battery prices per kWh are slightly lower than for battery electric cars, thanks to their larger size and therefore the reduced contribution of the battery pack cost». On charging, CALSTART writes about EVs in general (its examples are passenger EVs) and describes ultra-fast charging as «shrinking charging times from hours to 30 minutes or even less», and says «The appeal of 800V is no longer a dream and may become a reality for fleets across the industry craving more power for their vehicles.» *Analytical inference:* the charging statements are not truck results; the sources do not state a charging time, range, payload or cargo vertical for any electric truck. Gap: no source applies battery electric propulsion to an air or an ocean freight mode, so the sources give no verdict on air or ocean, and no cargo vertical (for example temperature-controlled, high-value or project freight) is addressed.\n\n## 6. Conversational and Strategic Talking Points\n\n- Say that, per the IEA, the life cycle emissions of a «medium-size battery electric car are half the emissions of an equivalent internal combustion engine (ICE) car as a global average»; that is a statement about cars, so do not present it as a truck result.\n- Ask a road subcontractor which battery chemistry its electric trucks use: the IEA states NMC batteries «still provide an energy density advantage, though the gap has narrowed in recent years», while LFP is the cheaper one.\n- Avoid quoting a truck cost parity year: none of the sources gives one.\n\n## 7. Time-to-Market, Procurement Window, and Action\n\nMONITORING: the sources do not give a date by which electric trucks are on sale at scale or by which a fleet has to commit; the IEA statement is that electric trucks reach more than 8% of EV battery demand by 2030. Procurement window gap: no source states when electric trucks are commercially available at scale.\n\n- A road procurement lead, at the next quarterly review of regional haulage capacity, records the IEA electric truck battery demand figures (nearly 3% in 2024, more than 8% by 2030) and the 2024 pack price falls beside the current subcontractor rate card. *Operational implication:* this gives the review a battery cost baseline to test supplier electric truck offers against.\n- A subcontractor manager asks each road haulier, in the next tender round, which battery chemistry, charging time and delivery lead time its electric truck offer rests on, and files the answers against the IEA LFP and NMC cost difference.\n\n- 19 December 2024: IEA publishes the EV Battery Supply Chain Sustainability report («Published 19 December 2024»).\n- February 27, 2026: CALSTART post on EV battery trends and 2026 expectations.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- International Energy Agency, Global EV Outlook 2025, Electric vehicle batteries: https://www.iea.org/reports/global-ev-outlook-2025/electric-vehicle-batteries\n- International Energy Agency, EV Battery Supply Chain Sustainability: https://www.iea.org/reports/ev-battery-supply-chain-sustainability\n- Carnegie Mellon University College of Engineering, Anticipating and reducing EV battery supply disruptions: https://engineering.cmu.edu/news-events/news/2024/03/19-ev-supply-chain.html\n- CALSTART, Top 10 EV Battery Trends in 2025 and What We Can Expect in 2026: https://calstart.org/top-10-ev-battery-trends-in-2025-and-what-we-can-expect-in-2026-february-27-2026/\n",
