   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThis item is a progress report, not an experiment: the Lloyd's Register Maritime Decarbonisation Hub (the Decarb Hub) reporting on its own work. The publisher describes itself in these words: «The Lloyd’s Register Maritime Decarbonisation Hub (The Decarb Hub) is an independent, non-profit collaboration between Lloyd’s Register Foundation and Lloyd’s Register Group, driving evidence-based action for zero-emission shipping.» Its scope is stated as «Our Year Four Impact Report gives an overview of 15 major initiatives across fuel adoption , risk and safety , and stakeholder impact aimed at turning ambition into measurable progress.»\n\nAmong the results it reports for fuel adoption: «Expanded The Silk Alliance green-corridor cluster to 19 members, advancing regional collaboration on methanol and ammonia adoption.» For fuel readiness it reports that «The Zero Carbon Fuel Monitor was relaunched with expanded coverage, providing an independent benchmark for fuel readiness across ammonia, methanol, hydrogen, batteries, and nuclear.» For safety it reports work «underpinned by quantitative risk assessments (QRAs)» on ammonia-fuelled vessels.\n\nThe report also gives its own totals: «£2.7 million in external funding secured £347,000 in donated expertise and services 16 programmes delivered across five impact pillars». These are figures about the Hub's activity and funding, not about emissions saved.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\nFor a forwarder, the practical point is what carriers will be able to offer. A green-corridor cluster of 19 members and a public benchmark of fuel readiness are signals about which ship fuels, such as methanol and ammonia, are being organised into services. The source gives no corridor route, sailing date, fuel price or freight rate, so no effect on a booking, a surcharge or a transit time is stated.\n\n*Operational implication:* when a client asks whether a carrier's low-emission service is real, the Zero Carbon Fuel Monitor is a named place to check how ready the fuel is, and the Silk Alliance membership is a named group of parties in a green corridor; neither is shown here to change cost or schedule.\n\nA separate vendor page in the same group describes a data product: «Seasearcher brings together voyage-level CO₂ estimates, CII and AER ratings, and fleet benchmarking in a single platform». It is the vendor's marketing for its own product, and it makes the claim «today, 60% of shipowners are failing to file EU emissions reports on time.» That figure is the vendor's statement and the page gives no study behind it, so it is not used here as evidence.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\nThe decision relevance is about what a forwarder can claim. It can say that an independent benchmark for ammonia, methanol, hydrogen, battery and nuclear fuel readiness exists and was relaunched. It cannot say, from this report, that the Hub's work has cut emissions on any trade lane, because no emissions figure is reported.\n\nThe report also states where the Hub goes next: «In the next phase (2024–2026), the Decarb Hub will: Advance green-corridor implementation and fuel-production business cases Strengthen human safety, competency, and training frameworks». *Analytical inference:* a forwarder following green corridors should expect further announcements from this group through 2026, resting on that stated plan.\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nThe methodology limits are plain from the scope line: the report is an overview of 15 initiatives chosen and described by the Hub, with no outside review stated. The evidence agreement signal is absent: the page calls the Hub «an independent, non-profit collaboration» but does not say the results were peer reviewed or checked by anyone else. The source authority signal is the publisher line above, a collaboration between Lloyd’s Register Foundation and Lloyd’s Register Group.\n\nOne claim cannot be tested from the pool: «Strategic advisory contributions helped catalyse hundreds of millions of dollars in sustainable ocean-finance mechanisms». It gives no amount, mechanism or date, so it is not a number a client can be given. The pages for the Zero Carbon Fuel Monitor and the Hub programme held no readable text beyond cookie notices, so what the Monitor currently shows for any fuel is not stated.\n\n- 15 April 2025: Decarb Hub Impact Report Year Four published, «Research report 15 April 2025»\n- 2026: end of the Hub's stated next phase, «In the next phase (2024–2026)»\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- thedecarbhub.org: https://www.thedecarbhub.org/insights-and-resources/decarb-hub-year-four-impact-report/\n- lloydslistintelligence.com: https://www.lloydslistintelligence.com/products/seasearcher/emissions\n- lr.org, decarbonisation: https://lr.org/en/sustainability/decarbonisation\n- lr.org, Zero Carbon Fuel Monitor: https://www.lr.org/en/knowledge/research/zcfm/\n- lrfoundation.org.uk: https://www.lrfoundation.org.uk/programmes/lloyds-register-maritime-decarbonisation-hub",
