   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThis is a journal study of how far the UK Government follows the advice of its climate advisory body, the Climate Change Committee (CCC). It was written by authors at the School of Environmental Sciences and the Tyndall Centre, University of East Anglia, and published in the journal Climate Policy. The authors report that «A systematic content analysis of government responses to 592 of the CCC’s 700 recommendations (2009–2020) finds that 23% were accepted, of which only 2% were accepted in full.»\n\nThe study is «taking written acceptance as a proxy for policy impact», so it measures what the government said in reply, not what was later done. It also reports that the form of a recommendation is linked to the answer: «For mitigation recommendations, those with a cross-sectoral focus were over four-times more likely to be accepted than those addressing a specific sector.» and «For those addressing adaptation, the only predictor of acceptance was the degree of repetition; repeated recommendations were nearly five-times more likely to be accepted than fresh ones.»\n\nTwo other items sit alongside it. A British Academy Innovation Fellowship, held by a Tyndall Centre researcher working with the Government Office for Science (GO-Science), is listed as «Fellowship with Government Office for Science (GO-Science) Jenkins, K. (Principal Investigator) British Academy 1/03/26 → 28/02/27». The government reply was «Government response to the Climate Change Committee 2025 adaptation progress report Published 29 October 2025». It is a separate document from the study. It says «The government is investing £15 million in UKRI-led research programmes to improve understanding of climate vulnerability and adaptive capacity across government and society.» That is research funding, not a transport adaptation programme.\n\nThe title this item carries names social vulnerability in UK adaptation decision-making, but none of the pages held states a finding on embedding social vulnerability; the Fellowship line gives only its dates and partner.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\nThe study measures only how the government replied in writing to the Committee’s recommendations for 2009 to 2020; it does not measure delivery, and it does not cover the later transport items below. The government reply is a separate document and names transport directly: «DfT launched Climate Risk Assessment Guidance in March 2025 to enable transport infrastructure operators to identify their climate risks and prioritise adaptation actions.» The decision relevance for a forwarder is that the strategy behind that guidance is not finished: «A finalised strategy will be published in due course.»\n\n*Operational implication:* a forwarder that plans UK routings on the assumption of a funded, finished adaptation programme for ports or roads is relying on a strategy the government reply describes as still to be finalised. The same researcher lists an article on port adaptation: «Climate change adaptation in the port industry: Evaluating evidence of implemented adaptation using a national adaptation inventory». The sources do not state a delay, a cost or a route affected, so no operational impact is quantified here.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\nThe finding changes what can be said about government replies to advice: it measures written acceptance of 592 recommendations for 2009 to 2020, and the study does not test any current adaptation announcement. The methodology limits are the authors’ own: «Further research should, however, also explore other factors that influence government responses as well as assess how far accepted recommendations are implemented.» For client conversations and tenders, a statement that UK transport infrastructure is being made climate-resilient can be backed by the published guidance and strategy status, but not by the acceptance figures, which describe replies to past recommendations and not delivery.\n\n*Analytical inference:* the guidance and the unfinished strategy in the government reply are what a forwarder can ask a UK port or road operator about; the acceptance figures do not tell which parts of any operator’s plan are funded or dated.\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nThe study does not show whether accepted recommendations were carried out. The Fellowship page lists the project dates as «1/03/26 → 28/02/27» and no output from it, so what it will find is open. The scope of the government’s research funding is not set out in the passage quoted above, so what it will study and when it reports is open. On the evidence agreement signal, the authors present a new method on one case: «The fact that the CCC is the oldest such body in the world makes it an obvious case to develop and test empirically a new method for undertaking assessments of policy impact.» The source authority signal is the publisher line «Climate Policy Volume 26, 2026 - Issue 2 Submit an article Journal homepage Open access», with the authors at the University of East Anglia. These sources hold no independent repeat of the study.\n\n- 29 October 2025: government response to the adaptation progress report published\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- research-portal.uea.ac.uk: https://research-portal.uea.ac.uk/en/persons/katie-jenkins/\n- tandfonline.com: https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14693062.2025.2497881\n- gov.uk, government response to the Climate Change Committee 2025 adaptation progress report: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/government-response-to-the-climate-change-committee-2025-adaptation-progress-report/government-response-to-the-climate-change-committee-2025-adaptation-progress-report\n- gov.uk, Climate Adaptation Research and Innovation Framework: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/climate-adaptation-research-and-innovation-framework/climate-adaptation-research-and-innovation-framework",
