   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThe research is the International Energy Agency (IEA) Global EV Outlook 2024. The IEA's page dates it: «Published 23 April 2024» The page describes it as: «The Global EV Outlook is an annual publication that identifies and assesses recent developments in electric mobility across the globe.»\n\nThe headline finding for freight is on electric trucks: «Sales of electric trucks increased 35% in 2023 compared to 2022, meaning that total sales of electric trucks surpassed electric buses for the first time, reaching around 54 000.» Where the sales happen: «China is the leading market for electric trucks, accounting for 70% of global sales in 2023, down from 85% in 2022.» «In Europe, electric truck sales increased almost threefold in 2023 to reach more than 10 000» «The United States also saw a threefold increase, though electric truck sales reached just 1 200, less than 0.1% of total truck sales.»\n\nThe IEA puts this in context of the whole sector: «Although they represent fewer than 8% of vehicles (excluding two- and three-wheelers), trucks and buses are responsible for more than 35% of direct CO 2 emissions from road transport.» It rates progress: «Tracking Trucks and Buses Not on track» The target it measures against: «To align with the Net Zero Emissions by 2050 (NZE) Scenario, emissions must fall by 15% from 2022 to 2030, declining at roughly 2% per year.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the 35% rise is a rise from a small base, since electric truck sales are about 54 000 in 2023 and the United States figure is less than 0.1% of total truck sales, so the growth rate and the share tell different stories. Methodology and limitations: the IEA states its data basis: «IEA analysis based on country submissions and data from EV Volumes, as well as China EV100, CADA, CCVDA and Interact Analysis for sales data for China.» The page also says the outlook is a mix of past data and projections: «Combining analysis of historical data with projections – now extended to 2035» The held pages give no error range for the sales figures.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\nThe IEA reports where policy is pushing road freight: «In May 2024, the European Union adopted ambitious CO 2 standards for most new trucks and coaches (90% emissions reduction by 2040) and urban buses (100% zero-emission sales by 2035).» «In March 2024, the United States released the National Zero-Emission Freight Corridor Strategy , which sets out a phased approach to establishing charging and refuelling infrastructure for zero-emission medium- and heavy-duty vehicles.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the EU standard and the United States corridor strategy are government measures aimed at trucks and charging; the IEA text does not say they reach a freight rate or a surcharge, and it names no cost to a forwarder. What it supports is a direction for road haulage supply: more electric trucks and more charging along corridors over the stated years.\n\nOn cost, the IEA says: «electric trucks are already competitive with diesel trucks in terms of total cost of ownershiplifetime costs, especially when charged at depot as opposed to higher-cost public charging.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* this concerns medium-duty trucks in North America where charged at a depot, so it does not show that electric trucks are cheaper on long hauls or in other regions.\n\nCity access is another pressure point: «zones for freight , are being implemented in 15 different cities as diverse as London, Los Angeles, Madrid, Quito, Shenzhen, Seoul and Taoyuan City. Together, these cities have a total population of over 52 million.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* delivery into those cities may be subject to local zone rules; the IEA lists the number of cities and not their rules, so any effect on a carrier's routing is not stated.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\n*Analytical inference:* on claims, a statement that electric trucks are scaling is supported for 2023, with China at 70% of global sales, Europe above 10 000 trucks and the United States at 1 200. A claim that electric trucks are widely available to a carrier in the United States is not supported, because the US share is less than 0.1%.\n\nOn decisions, the IEA states its expectation, and the US rule: «We expect electric truck sales to continue to increase thanks to strong and ambitious policies» «In the United States, the newly adopted heavy-duty emissions regulation is expected to result in ZEV sales shares of up to 60% by 2032 in different segments.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* an operator choosing between a diesel and an electric truck tender can use the TCO sentence in section 2 as a reason to ask for a depot-charging quote, and the sales ranges as a reason to expect more models over time. The pages give no price.\n\nOn India, the IEA reports: «This initiative has spurred 16 major manufacturing and logistics companies to collectively signal demand for 7&nbsp;750 electric freight vehicles by 2030.» It adds the conditions: «To meet this demand, focused efforts will be needed on policy regulation, establishing market certainty, scalable pilot support, infrastructure development, and creating blended financing platforms to attract private investments.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* a demand signal is not an order book; the sentence says companies signalled demand, and it states no purchases. In the United Kingdom, a trial programme is described: «the UK Government is investing USD&nbsp;250&nbsp;million under the Zero Emission HGV and Infrastructure Programme to run real-world trials of zero-emission trucks. The programme will deploy 370 trucks and almost 60 refuelling and electric charging sites.»\n\n## 4. Client Conversation Talking Points and Public Position\n\n*Analytical inference:* what the workspace can say is that the IEA reports electric truck sales rose 35% in 2023 and that government standards in the EU and United States are aimed at further growth. A question to put to a client with road legs is whether its hauliers are buying or trialling electric trucks and where they charge.\n\n*Analytical inference:* pitfalls are to quote the 35% growth without the small base, to apply a 2023 figure to a later year, or to present the Electric Vehicles Initiative's goals as the IEA's finding. The initiative's own page describes itself this way: «The Electric Vehicles Initiative (EVI) is a multi-government policy forum dedicated to accelerating the introduction and adoption of electric vehicles worldwide.»\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nThe page now shows a newer edition: «Global EV Outlook 2026 available» so the 2023 sales figures are about three years old at the date of this brief and later sales are not held. Whether the 2025 and 2030 national targets listed on the Electric Vehicles Initiative page were met is also not held.\n\nOn how settled or contested the finding is, the held pages state no peer review, independent confirmation or dispute of the figures. The sector is described as not on track in the page quoted in section 1.\n\nOn who published or issued it: the outlook is «It is developed with the support of members of the Electric Vehicles Initiative (EVI).»\n\nThe government fleet commitments on the Electric Vehicles Initiative page are targets, for example: «The Australian Public Service has committed to a target of net zero emissions by 2030, including a target requiring 75 per cent of passenger vehicle new leases or purchases under the Commonwealth Fleet arrangement to be low emission vehicles by 2025.» «Canada has also committed to achieve net-zero emissions by 2050 from its national safety and security operations, and by 2023 departments will develop and regularly update Operational Fleet Decarbonization Plans that outline how they will reduce their emissions in line with the 2050 target.» These are passenger and government fleet commitments and not freight fleet rules.\n\n- 2023: Canada's departments were to develop fleet decarbonization plans by this year (past; no result held), and the year of the IEA's sales data\n- 2025: Australian Public Service passenger vehicle target year (passed; no result held)\n- 2030: India 7 750 electric freight vehicle demand signal, and Australian Public Service net zero target\n- 2032: United States heavy-duty rule expected to give ZEV sales shares of up to 60%\n- 2035: EU target of 100% zero-emission city bus sales\n- 2040: EU truck CO2 standard reaching a 90% reduction\n- 2050: Canada net-zero operations target\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- www.iea.org: https://iea.org/reports/global-ev-outlook-2024\n- www.iea.org: https://www.iea.org/reports/global-ev-outlook-2024/trends-in-heavy-electric-vehicles\n- www.iea.org: https://www.iea.org/energy-system/transport/trucks-and-buses\n- www.cleanenergyministerial.org: https://www.cleanenergyministerial.org/initiatives-campaigns/electric-vehicles-initiative/",
