   "body": "## 1. What's Being Tested or Deployed and By Whom\n\nThis item is about which fuels and rules will carry international shipping to lower emissions, and the evidence is institutional rather than a single vessel trial. The International Maritime Organization (IMO) states that «IMO's short-term GHG reduction measures ( EEXI, CII rating ), developed to improve ship energy efficiency and reduce carbon intensity by at least 40% compared to 2008 levels by 2030, entered into force on 1 January 2023.» It adds that «ships get a rating of their energy efficiency (A, B, C, D, E – where A is the best). A ship running on a low carbon fuel gets a higher rating than one running on fossil fuel.» The European Commission reports that «The regulation is fully applied from 1 January 2025 except for articles 8 and 9 on monitoring plans that are applied from August 2024.» and that «The first FuelEU report submission to a verifier is due on 31 January 2026, containing the data from 1 January 2025 to 31 December 2025.» The Lloyd's Register Maritime Decarbonisation Hub published a research report, listed as «Research report 11 September 2023», with fuel mix projections rather than deployments. The IMO lists the candidate fuels: «Potential future fuels and propulsion for shipping include ammonia, biofuels, electric power, fuel cells, hydrogen, methanol, and wind.» *Analytical inference:* the sources show two regimes already in operation, the IMO's energy efficiency rating and the EU's fuel GHG intensity limit, and no ship operator with a quantified switch to a new fuel; the fuels themselves are described by scenario. Gap: no source names a shipping company or vessel class with a measured fuel result.\n\n## 2. What This Tells Us About Industry Trajectory\n\nThe direction is set by the IMO strategy adopted on «07 July 2023» by member states, with the IMO stating that «The Strategy includes indicative check-points for international shipping to reach net-zero GHG emissions for 2030 (by at least 20%, striving for 30%) and 2040 (by at least 70%, striving for 80%) compared to 2008» and, for fuels, that «they are to represent at least 5%, striving for 10%, of the energy used by international shipping by 2030.» On carbon intensity, the IMO envisages a reduction «as an average across international shipping, by at least 40% by 2030.» The measures to deliver this are two: «a technical element , namely a goal-based marine fuel standard regulating the phased reduction of the marine fuel's GHG intensity;» and «an economic element , on the basis of a maritime GHG emissions pricing mechanism.» The IMO states that its Marine Environment Protection Committee (MEPC) «finalized a draft legal text for these measures, referred to as the \" IMO Net-Zero Framework \", in April 2025.» The latest development on the same page is that «In October 2025, IMO's Marine Environment Protection Committee (MEPC) adjourned discussions on the adoption of draft amendments to MARPOL Annex VI, including the IMO Net-Zero Framework.» and the page notes «IMO net-zero shipping talks to resume in 2026», dated «(October 2026)». The IMO timeline is: «MEPC 85 (Autumn 2026) MEPC 86 (Summer 2027) - Initiate the review of the 2023 IMO GHG Strategy MEPC 87 (Spring 2028) MEPC 88 (Autumn 2028) - Finalization of the review of the 2023 IMO GHG Strategy with a view to adoption of the 2028 IMO Strategy on reduction of GHG emissions from ships.» *Analytical inference:* the IMO has set targets and a method but has not, in the sources, adopted the pricing and fuel standard measures; the EU rule is in operation now, so European port calls are where fuel GHG intensity is already regulated while the global rule is pending.\n\n## 3. Supplier Access and Procurement Reality\n\nThe Lloyd's Register Hub report puts numbers on supply. For hydrogen based fuels: «E-ammonia is expected to capture an average of 35% of the global marine fuel mix by 2050.» «When combined with blue ammonia, adoption could range between 20–60%, with demand projected to grow from 0.79 EJ in 2030 to 6.06 EJ in 2050.» For biofuels: the report says «liquefied bio-methane, could account for around 34% of the fuel mix by 2050. However, supply is unlikely to keep pace with demand, with forecasts of only 0.3–2 EJ available compared to 4.58 EJ required.» For methanol: the report projects «~13.4% of demand by 2050. Supply constraints are expected to limit its role in the long-term fuel mix.» The Hub concludes that «No single fuel is guaranteed to dominate, and the risks of supply gaps and high capital costs are significant.» The IMO adds that «There is great potential for developing countries to become key suppliers of zero and near zero carbon fuels for shipping, and other technologies.» and that «For different fuel options, there is a need to consider issues such as safety, regulation, pricing, infrastructural availability, lifecycle emissions, supply chain constraints, barriers to adoption and more.» The Commission describes how the EU rule lets owners choose: «The regulation also provides for different flexibility mechanisms, supporting existing fleets to find suitable compliance strategies and rewarding first-movers for early investment in energy transition.» *Analytical inference:* the scenario numbers show supply, not demand, as the limit for bio-methane and methanol, while ammonia is the fuel the Hub expects to scale; a carrier choosing a fuel is therefore choosing under supply risk that the Hub says is significant. Gap: no source gives a fuel price, a delivery lead time, a named supplier or a bunkering location for any of these fuels, so who can buy at what scale is stated only as scenario shares.\n\n## 4. Operational Fit by Transport Mode and Cargo Vertical\n\nOcean is the only mode the sources reach. The Commission says that «FuelEU maritime sets maximum limits for the yearly average greenhouse gas (GHG) intensity of the energy used by ships above 5,000 gross tonnage calling at European ports, regardless of their flag.» and that «The targets cover not only CO 2 but also methane and nitrous oxide emissions over the full lifecycle of the fuels used onboard, on a well-to-wake (WtW) basis.» On pace, it states: «Targets will ensure that the greenhouse gas intensity of fuels used in the sector will gradually decrease over time, starting with a 2% decrease by 2025 and reaching up to an 80% reduction by 2050.» The yearly path it lists is «Annual average carbon intensity reduction compared to the average in 2020: 2025: -2%; 2030: -6%;» with the end point «2050: -80%.» For vessels at berth, the Commission writes that «passenger and container ships at berth or moored at the quayside must use on-shore power supply (OPS) or alternative zero-emission technologies from 1 January 2030 onwards in ports covered under article 9 of the alternative fuels infrastructure regulation (AFIR), and from 1 January 2035 in all EU ports that develop OPS capacity.» The IMO adds the life cycle view: «These allow for an assessment of greenhouse gas emissions from the fuel production to the ship (Well-to-Wake)» *Analytical inference:* the link between fuel, ship and shipper runs through the carrier: a vessel above the tonnage line calling in Europe is measured on the GHG intensity of the energy it uses, and a passenger or container ship at berth in a covered port is measured on its shore power connection; the sources do not state how any carrier passes these costs to a shipper. *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether and from when the FuelEU limits and the on-shore power duties fall on a given ship or port call, a question for counsel. Gap: the sources give no verdict for air or road, and no cargo vertical or lane is addressed.\n\n## 6. Conversational and Strategic Talking Points\n\n- Say that the IMO's 2023 strategy aims for «they are to represent at least 5%, striving for 10%, of the energy used by international shipping by 2030.»\n- Ask a carrier which of its European port calls fall under the EU fuel GHG intensity limit, using the Commission's wording: «FuelEU maritime sets maximum limits for the yearly average greenhouse gas (GHG) intensity of the energy used by ships above 5,000 gross tonnage calling at European ports, regardless of their flag.»\n- Avoid presenting the IMO pricing and fuel standard as adopted: the IMO states that «In October 2025, IMO's Marine Environment Protection Committee (MEPC) adjourned discussions on the adoption of draft amendments to MARPOL Annex VI, including the IMO Net-Zero Framework.»\n\n## 7. Time-to-Market, Procurement Window, and Action\n\nMONITORING: the dated points in the sources are the EU reporting steps already passed and the IMO decision still pending. The IMO page gives no date by which the net-zero framework is adopted; it states «IMO net-zero shipping talks to resume in 2026», and the Hub gives no date when low-carbon fuels are available at scale.\n\n- An ocean procurement lead, at the next carrier tender covering European port calls, asks each carrier to state which fuel it is using to meet the percentage path the Commission lists («2050: -80%.» being the 2050 end point) and records the answer beside the Hub's fuel share scenarios. *Operational implication:* this shows which carriers are exposed to the supply limits the Hub describes.\n- A trade-lane manager asks each carrier for the outcome of its first FuelEU report, which the Commission says was due to a verifier on 31 January 2026, and files whether a surplus or a deficit was reported.\n- A sustainability lead re-reads the IMO hot topics page after the Autumn 2026 MEPC session named in its timeline and updates the client position on whether the IMO measures have been adopted.\n\n- 1 January 2023: IMO short-term measure (EEXI and CII rating) entered into force.\n- 1 January 2025: FuelEU Maritime fully applied, with a 2% GHG intensity decrease by 2025.\n- 31 December 2025: end of the first FuelEU reporting period.\n- 1 January 2026: IMO timeline entry, «Review of the short-term measure to be completed by 1 January 2026».\n- 1 January 2030: on-shore power supply duty starts in ports covered under article 9 of the alternative fuels infrastructure regulation.\n- 1 January 2035: on-shore power supply duty extends to all EU ports that develop the capacity.\n- 2040: IMO indicative check-point of at least 70% GHG reduction compared to 2008.\n- 2050: net-zero ambition, and FuelEU -80% end point.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- International Maritime Organization, IMO's work to cut GHG emissions from ships: https://imo.org/en/mediacentre/hottopics/pages/cutting-ghg-emissions.aspx\n- International Maritime Organization, Revised GHG reduction strategy for global shipping adopted: https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/revised-ghg-reduction-strategy-for-global-shipping-adopted-.aspx\n- European Commission, Decarbonising maritime transport, FuelEU Maritime: https://transport.ec.europa.eu/transport-modes/maritime/decarbonising-maritime-transport-fueleu-maritime_en\n- Lloyd's Register Maritime Decarbonisation Hub, The Future of Maritime Fuels: https://www.thedecarbhub.org/insights-and-resources/the-future-of-maritime-fuels/\n- MDPI Sustainability, Technical Requirements for 2023 IMO GHG Strategy (title only held): https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/16/7/2766\n",
