   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThis item is a research overview from the Fraunhofer Institute for Material Flow and Logistics IML in Germany, drawn mainly from its own magazine, with a trade fair report and a partner press release. It sets out how digital tools, automation and decarbonisation are changing logistics. On adoption, the magazine says: «In 2022, around 22% of German logistics companies were already actively using AI, with a further 26% planning or discussing its use.» On emissions it says: «The transport sector is responsible for around 22% of CO2 emissions in Germany, a third of which is attributable to freight transport.» It adds that «Around 62% of logistics companies rely on digital technologies to reduce emissions and make processes more sustainable.»\n\nOn supply chain visibility it reports: «While 79% of companies at least monitor the first tier of suppliers, only 14% also monitor the lower levels of their supply chains.» A market forecast is also given: the global market volume for green logistics is forecast to reach «around 1.5 billion US dollars by 2028».\n\nOn packaging, a Fraunhofer decarbonisation page describes a software project in these terms: «With an eye toward the European Packaging and Packaging Waste Regulation (PPWR), which mandates a minimum volume utilization of 50% starting in 2030, CASTN provides transparency regarding volume utilization and offers data-driven optimization options.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the PPWR requirement is Fraunhofer's description on a project page; the regulation text is not among the sources, so its scope is not restated here beyond that sentence.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\nFor a freight forwarder, the practical content is the set of tools the institute presents for forwarders and shippers. Its trade fair announcement, for «Transport Logistic 2025 from June 2 to 5 in Munich» (a fair that is now past; the pages held do not say how the tools were received), says: «Among other things, a AI platform for freight forwarders, digital product passports for supply chains, and tools for emissions assessment will be showcased.» The emissions tool is described as «an application for assessing greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions from handling and storage, as well as GHG emissions from logistics sites worldwide», and the page adds «Participation in the ISO standard 14083 for emissions assessment is also included.»\n\n*Operational implication:* a forwarder asked by a client for emissions data on warehouse handling and storage can name this web tool as one tool the institute presents for producing it, and can ask clients whether they face CSRD reporting obligations, which the trade fair text names as a focus. The sources give no price, accuracy test or user numbers for the tool, so no cost or reliability claim is made.\n\nThe air cargo item matters to forwarders handling air freight: the fair report mentions a standard of a different body, not a step of this research: «the IATA data standard \"ONE Record,\" which is intended to enable more efficient data integration in air freight starting in 2026».\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\nThe decision relevance is about where to invest attention. The magazine names the hurdles: «New technologies not only require high initial investments, but also qualified HR officers who can handle the new systems.» and «volatile markets, geopolitical crises and a shortage of specialists also act as a brake on innovation.» For client conversations, the survey-style figures can be quoted as the institute's, and not as measurements of any particular fleet or customer base.\n\n*Analytical inference:* because the 79% and 14% figures describe how far companies monitor their suppliers, a forwarder with deep-tier supplier clients can ask whether they track beyond the first tier, resting on the 14% figure above; the source does not say which sectors or countries those firms are in.\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nThe methodology limits are plain: the main source calls itself «This article was published in ”discover LOGISTICS”, our in-house and customer magazine, which informs you about current events and the results of applied research and development work.» It gives no sample size, survey date or method for most of its figures, apart from naming the FM Resilience Index 2025 for one ranking. The evidence agreement signal is absent: the pages do not say the figures were peer reviewed or confirmed by another study. The source authority signal is the publisher, the Fraunhofer Institute for Material Flow and Logistics IML.\n\nThe pool names no country for the 62%, 79% and 14% figures. The adoption figures are for German companies in 2022 («In 2022, around 22% of German logistics companies were already actively using AI») and say nothing about forwarders elsewhere. A partner press release adds one development of limited reach for forwarders: «The core of the collaboration is a three-year joint “Enterprise Lab” for the development of mobile robots.»\n\n- 17 March 2026: Fraunhofer IML and BEUMER Group collaboration announced, «Beckum, 17 March 2026»\n- 2028: forecast year for the green logistics market, «by 2028»\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- iml.fraunhofer.de, automation: https://www.iml.fraunhofer.de/en/topics/automation.html\n- iml.fraunhofer.de, decarbonization: https://www.iml.fraunhofer.de/en/topics/more-efficiency-through-new-technologies-sustainability-in-logistics/decarbonization.html\n- iml.fraunhofer.de, discover LOGISTICS issue 26: https://www.iml.fraunhofer.de/en/press-and-media/discover-logistics/discover-logistics-26/logistics-of-the-future-more-intelligent-more-resilient-more-sustainable.html\n- beumergroup.com: https://www.beumergroup.com/press/beumer-group-and-fraunhofer-institute-for-material-flow-and-logistics-iml-launch-joint-enterprise-lab-for-mobile-robotics/\n- nfz-messe.com: https://www.nfz-messe.com/en/news/transport-logistic-digitalisierung-und-vernetzung-transport-logistic-2025-fraunhofer-iml-presents-solutions-ai-resilience-and-sustainability-4680.html",
