   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThe held pages are a research programme overview, not a study with a result. They describe the Transportation and Mobility Research programme of the National Laboratory of the Rockies (NLR), a U.S. Department of Energy laboratory, and the Department of Energy's Transportation Technologies Office that funds this field. The programme states its aim as «NLR pioneers world-class research accelerating the development of affordable, reliable, and efficient transportation and mobility technologies to move people and goods across America.»\n\nThe programme describes its integration work as «Drawing on more than 30 years of transportation research experience, NLR provides data, tools, and technical assistance to support transportation technology evaluation and planning decisions.» Its stated approach is «This integrated, data-informed approach ensures the adoption of transportation energy choices that result in lower operational costs, improved mobility, and better air quality.»\n\nThe methodology limits are those of an overview page. The pages name research areas, tools and partners, and give no sample, test result or measured saving. The laboratory's own research repository page for this topic lists «Research Output (1)», a single listed paper titled «Two-Phase Spray Cooling of Hybrid Vehicle Electronics», which is a vehicle electronics paper and not a freight finding. The headline findings of the programme are therefore not stated in the held pages.\n\n*Analytical inference:* the programme page is evidence of what the laboratory does and for whom, not evidence that any particular technology lowers a fleet's cost.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\n*Analytical inference:* the programme matters to a forwarder as a source of planning tools and neutral technical help on vehicles, fuels and charging, not as a source of a rate or a rule. The tools the page lists include «NLR's flagship suite of tools that inform the planning and development of large-scale electric vehicle charging infrastructure deployments» and a data platform it describes as «The world's only secure, anonymized, national commercial vehicle data platform.» It also names a charging cost topic: «NLR conducts EV charging soft costs analysis to uncover potential cost reductions and opportunities to streamlines processes and permitting.»\n\nThe Department of Energy office page gives the freight context in its own words: «The transportation system’s trucks, ships, trains, and planes move 55 million tons of goods, worth more than $49 billion, and transport people across 3 trillion vehicle miles annually.» The page gives no period for the goods figure, so it is not read here as a yearly total. It also names a community cost of transport: «contributes to air pollution which increases the risk for asthma, heart disease, and other health issues, especially in communities near highways, ports, rail yards, and warehouses.»\n\n*Operational implication:* a forwarder with road fleets, or with warehouses and ports in its network, operates in the places the Department of Energy page names as bearing pollution costs. The pages do not state that this creates any obligation or cost for a forwarder. They name states, fleets, municipalities, Tribal Nations, school districts and transit agencies as partners, and a forwarder is not named among them.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\nWhat it changes is where to look for planning tools, not what a forwarder must do. The programme says its tools turn research into usable services: «NLR's renowned tools and data experts support market-driven adoption and use of alternative fuels and advanced vehicles by simplifying complex models into user-friendly web tools and services, making research data more accessible.» One listed tool is «Comprehensive database of alternative fueling stations in the United States and Canada», and another, REopt, is described as a «Web-based tool used in conjunction with electric vehicle charging system analyses.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those descriptions, a forwarder that is weighing electric trucks or charging at its own sites can use these listed tools as inputs to the decision, and can say it used a Department of Energy laboratory's planning tools. It cannot say the laboratory has shown that electrification lowers its cost, because the pages report no such result. The pages also mention programmes that help fleets with legal provisions: the laboratory manages a Department of Energy program that «works with covered fleets to help them meet provisions in the Energy Policy Act.» That addresses state and alternative fuel provider fleets, and the pages do not say a forwarder is one.\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nWhat is not resolved is every measured outcome. The programme itself says its work is tailored: «Responsive research is the key component of NLR's transportation initiatives, with a recognition that solutions should address individual transportation goals.» The pages give no result that carries over from one fleet to another.\n\nOn how settled the work is, the pages state no language on peer review, independent confirmation or dispute of the programme's tools or analysis, so the evidence-agreement signal is not stated.\n\nOn who issued it, the page says «The National Laboratory of the Rockies is a national laboratory of the U.S. Department of Energy, Office of Critical Minerals and Energy Innovation». The Department of Energy office page says «The Transportation Technologies Office (TTO) funds research, development, demonstration, and deployment of innovative, efficient, and affordable transportation solutions».\n\nThe one dated programme step in the held pages is a conference showing:\n\n- Jan. 2026: NLR showed research at a Transportation Research Board meeting, in the page's words «NLR Showcases Research at Transportation Research Board Meeting, Jan. 11–15, 2026» (past)\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- nlr.gov: https://nlr.gov/transportation\n- www.nlr.gov: https://www.nlr.gov/transportation/research\n- www.nlr.gov: https://www.nlr.gov/transportation/transportation-technology-integration\n- www.energy.gov: https://www.energy.gov/cmei/vehicles/vehicle-technologies-office\n- research-hub.nlr.gov: https://research-hub.nlr.gov/en/organisations/transportation-and-mobility-research-topic/\n",
