   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThis item groups work from the International Transport Forum (ITF), the OECD's transport body, on how fast freight emissions are likely to grow and what could slow them. The headline finding comes from its Decarbonising Transport initiative: «Transport emits around 23% of the energy-related CO 2 that feeds global warming.» and «As a result CO 2 emissions from transport activity will not fall, but could increase by 60% by 2050.» It also warns that «Without imm ediate action, its share could reach 40% by 2030.» The reason it gives is dependence on one fuel: «And because transport relies on oil for 92% of its energy, it is particularly hard to decarbonise».\n\nFor road freight the ITF report states: «Goods transport by road consumes around 50% of all diesel fuel and accounts for 80% of the global net increase in diesel use since 2000.» It adds: «Projections see road freight activity at least doubling to 2050, offsetting efficiency gains and increasing road freight CO2 emissions.» For Southeast Asia, the ITF reports: «The ITF’s global freight transport model predicts that demand for freight transport will more than double in Southeast Asia by 2050, placing considerable strain on infrastructure and service quality while contributing to rising carbon emissions.»\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\nFor a freight forwarder, the 2018 road freight report projects that road freight activity will grow so that efficiency gains are offset and its CO2 emissions increase; that is that report’s projection for road freight, dated 5 December 2018, not a finding of the 2025 Southeast Asia study. The Southeast Asia study says what it expects to work: «This project finds that policy measures and infrastructure investments focused on connectivity, decarbonisation and resilience complement one another, producing a regional freight transport system that is more competitive, efficient, environmentally-friendly and adaptable to disruptions.» The study covers «ten countries in Southeast Asia, with a particular focus on Thailand, Indonesia, and the Philippines».\n\n*Operational implication:* a forwarder moving cargo in or out of those countries can ask carriers and operators on those lanes how they plan for the strain on infrastructure and service quality that the model projects. The sources do not state a cost, a surcharge, a transit time or a route for any shipment, and do not name any carrier, so no commercial effect is quantified here.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\nThe decision relevance is in claims and planning. The road freight report says it «highlights policy areas that need adjustment for effective decarbonisation of road freight», so a forwarder asked about road-freight emissions can cite the ITF's own diesel figures but should not present them as a forecast for its own fleet or lanes. The ITF also states its role: «The Decarbonising Transport initiative does not advocate specific measures or policies.» *Analytical inference:* because the ITF offers options and not a ruling, a statement to clients that a given measure is required by the ITF is not supported by these pages.\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nThe methodology limits are those of modelling and a survey. The initiative says its assessments are «grounded in data analysis and advanced modelling», and the road freight report «collects insights held at a workshop organised by the International Transport Forum in June 2018 in Paris», so that part of its evidence base dates from 2018. The report itself flags open questions: it points to fields where «more robust evidence through further research is needed».\n\nThe Key Transport Statistics brief is explicit that its freight data are early: it offers «preliminary data for more than a dozen selected indicators on three inland transport modes for ITF member countries», and no freight volume or emission figure from it is used here. The evidence agreement signal is absent: the pages do not say the projections were peer reviewed or confirmed by other studies. The source authority signal is the publisher line «Publication Type Case-Specific Policy Analysis Publisher OECD/ITF».\n\n- 5 December 2018: road freight decarbonisation report published, «Date of Publication 5 December 2018»\n- 15 May 2025: Key Transport Statistics 2025 published, «Date of Publication 15 May 2025»\n- 24 July 2025: Southeast Asia freight connectivity study published, «Date of Publication 24 July 2025»\n- 2050: projection year for freight demand and transport emissions, «by 2050»\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- itf-oecd.org, Decarbonising Transport: https://www.itf-oecd.org/decarbonising-transport\n- itf-oecd.org, Towards Road Freight Decarbonisation: https://www.itf-oecd.org/towards-road-freight-decarbonisation\n- itf-oecd.org, Key Transport Statistics 2025: https://www.itf-oecd.org/key-transport-statistics-2025\n- itf-oecd.org, Southeast Asia freight connectivity: https://www.itf-oecd.org/southeast-asia-freight-connectivity\n- oecd.org, ITF Transport Outlook 2023: https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2023/05/itf-transport-outlook-2023_4466cd78.html",
