   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThe finding is a rating of the International Maritime Organization's climate policy for international shipping by the Climate Action Tracker (CAT), a page last listed with an update dated 26 Mar 2026. Headline: «The CAT rates 2030 emissions under current policies as “Highly Insufficient.”» The page speaks as the Climate Action Tracker: «The CAT rates 2030 emissions under current policies» The page explains the basis: «With the current level of policy action, CO 2 emissions are projected to be between 729-768 MtCO 2 in 2030 (based on a voyage-based approach), only representing a 1-6% decrease below the reference emissions level in 2008.»\n\n- Population and setting: international shipping, which the page sizes at «International shipping accounts for about 2.3% of global CO 2 emissions in 2023 (ICCT, 2025).»\n- Intervention: current policy action at the International Maritime Organization (IMO), and the Net Zero Framework (NZF) that was not adopted. «The IMO failed to adopt the Net Zero Framework (NZF), a global fuel standard and carbon pricing mechanism, at the Marine Environment Protection Committee’s (MEPC) extraordinary session in October 2025.»\n- Comparator: the reference emissions level in 2008 and 1.5°C compatible pathways. «There remains an ambition gap of 78-87% between current policy projections and 1.5°C compatible pathways for 2030.»\n- Outcome: «Current policies put the shipping sector far off track from achieving the IMO’s target of reducing emissions by 20% and striving for 30% by 2030, with an implementation gap of 109–186 MtCO 2 .»\n- Methodology limits: the rating is a conditional statement, not a measurement: «This rating indicates that the target is consistent with warming between 3°C and 4°C if all other sectors were to follow the same approach.» The projection uses «(based on a voyage-based approach)» The stored text of the page ends partway through the heavy fuel oil section, so later parts are not held.\n- Limitations and dissent as the page states them: «Instead, the EEXI and CII have a counterproductive impact on near-term ambition: the measures promote a switch to LNG-powered ships, resulting in greater GHG emissions» «It is unclear how the postponement will affect the agreed technical measures.»\n\n«The rating remains unchanged from the previous assessment since no new policy measures have been adopted that would reduce emissions projections.»\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\nFor ocean freight the page describes which rules are in force and which are not. The measures in force are efficiency measures: «The most recent measures, Energy Efficiency of Existing Ship Index (EEXI) and the Carbon Intensity Indicator (CII), came into effect in January 2023, with the first ratings and reporting issued in 2024» Ratings affect vessels the forwarder's carriers operate: «Ships rated E, or rated as D for three consecutive years, will need to develop a plan to increase their operational efficiency (DNV, 2023). In 2024, 78% of ships were awared a rating of C or higher»\n\nOn fuel, the page says the fleet's main approach so far has been efficiency, and gives its judgement of the result: «To date, the main approach implemented to reduce GHG emissions has been around improving the energy efficiency of ships to reduce carbon intensity» It also records the fuel direction the sector would need: «To achieve full decarbonisation, the shipping sector will need to adopt alternative fuels, otherwise known as scalable zero-emission fuels (SZEFs), to power vessels.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the cause is the IMO's failure to adopt the Net Zero Framework, which the page describes as a global fuel standard and carbon pricing mechanism; the consequence is that no new global fuel standard or shipping carbon price follows from it for now; the effect for a forwarder is that a cost from that mechanism is not stated to be coming, while the vote postponed to October 2026 leaves the question open. The page does not state how any shipping rule cost reaches a freight rate, a surcharge or an invoice, so no price effect is asserted, and it gives no result by cargo vertical.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\n- Sustainability claims: *Analytical inference:* resting on the CAT rating and the page's statement that the efficiency measures promote a switch to LNG-powered ships with greater GHG emissions, a claim that an ocean carrier's CII rating shows a falling climate impact is not supported by this source; the page states these measures have a counterproductive impact on near-term ambition. The page: «The NZF would have improved the CAT’s rating of the sector’s 2030 target and current policies from “Highly Insufficient” to “Insufficient.”»\n- Regulatory anticipation: the page records a postponed vote, not an adopted rule. «Instead of abandoning the NZF, governments decided to postpone the vote to October 2026.» *Analytical inference:* the page was last updated 26 Mar 2026, and the October 2026 month it names has passed; the stored text does not say what the member states decided in October 2026, so the outcome has to be checked before planning on either result.\n- Process: the page explains the route a measure takes: «Following adoption, a resolution will then need to be accepted by contracting party governments within an agreed fixed period, after which the resolution enters into force.» This matters because a measure adopted at the IMO still waits for acceptance before it applies to ships.\n- Vendor selection: the stored page names no supplier or tool.\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\n- Open question stated by the page: «It is unclear how the postponement will affect the agreed technical measures.»\n- Disagreement: the page criticises the efficiency approach, while the IMO adopted it as its main policy measure. «Instead, the EEXI and CII have a counterproductive impact on near-term ambition: the measures promote a switch to LNG-powered ships, resulting in greater GHG emissions»\n- Maturity and agreement: the rating is the CAT's own assessment. «The rating remains unchanged from the previous assessment since no new policy measures have been adopted that would reduce emissions projections.» The stored page cites sources such as the ICCT for the shipping share and gives no peer review statement.\n- Blockers stated: «Further policy implementation and finance mobilisation is necessary to achieve the enabling conditions to spearhead accelerated uptake in fuel production, port infrastructure and storage, and on-board technologies»\n- Stale forward statements: «an ‘economic instrument’ (i.e. a market-based measure) for adoption in 2025, with implementation expected from 2027.» The page then says: «As of October 2025, this failed to materialise through the postponed vote of the Net Zero Framework (NZF).» The stored text does not say what followed the October 2026 vote.\n\n*Analytical inference:* falsifiable signposts: if the postponed vote adopted the Net Zero Framework, the page's own statement that it would have improved the rating from \"Highly Insufficient\" to \"Insufficient\" gives the test of what to expect; the stored page does not say whether that happened.\n\nStrategy milestones the page records: «Agreed to a fuel-based target of at least 5%, striving for 10%, of the energy used in international shipping to come from zero and “near zero” emission fuels and technologies by 2030.» «Agreed to consider long-term measures beyond 2030 as part of the review of the Strategy in 2028.» «to reduce GHG emissions at least 50% below 2008 levels by 2050 (IMO, 2018)»\n\nThe page lists these updates: «Choose update to view 26 Mar 2026 12 Oct 2023 30 Jun 2023 19 Jul 2021 25 Jun 2020»\n\n- 26 Mar 2026: latest update listed on the page\n- October 2026: IMO member states' postponed vote on the Net Zero Framework, a month that has passed; the stored text does not give the result\n- 2027: implementation of an economic instrument had been expected from this year\n- 2028: review of the IMO Strategy\n- 2030: checkpoint year for the 20% emissions target and the 5% zero-emission fuel target\n- 2050: long-term year in the 2018 Initial Strategy, with the 2008 baseline\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- climateactiontracker.org: https://climateactiontracker.org/sectors/shipping/imo-policies-action/\n",
