   "source_pool_hash": "c6fb7612876681e182f78932b0dfd1ce17d8f3a364c880bc7ccdf7d35bfd92f6",
   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThe held sources are three McKinsey publications on the energy transition, and a McKinsey index page of sustainability articles: the Global Energy Perspective 2025 (published «October 13, 2025 | Report»), The hard stuff 2025 from the McKinsey Global Institute (published «November 6, 2025 | Article»), and a tracking article (published «January 14, 2026 | Article»). They are consulting research by one publisher, so their agreement is not independent confirmation.\n\nThe headline finding, stated in the tracking article for the nine technologies it evaluates across China, Europe and the United States, is «Today, less than 15 percent of the low-emissions technologies required to meet Paris-aligned targets by 2050 have been deployed, only a few percentage points higher than two years ago.» The Global Institute article gives the estimate and the pace: «We find that an estimated 13.5 percent of the deployment to meet Paris-aligned goals has now been done.» and «This pace is roughly half of what would be required to meet Paris-aligned targets. Six percentage points on deployment in the past two years would have been in line with such targets».\n\nThe Global Energy Perspective adds scenario results: «The expected temperature change by 2100 in our scenarios is 1.9°C in Sustainable Transformation, 2.3°C in Continued Momentum, and 2.7°C in Slow Evolution.» and a fuels result: «Crucial alternative fuels are not likely to achieve broad adoption before 2040 unless mandated.»\n\nThe methodology limits are stated by the authors. The tracking article says «In this article, we are neither modeling nor forecasting future outcomes but rather bringing to light the available data to assess the size of the reality gap and what needs to be done to close it.» Its scope is «we evaluate nine key decarbonization technologies across China, Europe (which in our analysis includes the European Union, Norway, Switzerland, and the United Kingdom), and the United States». The Global Institute article states its deployment method: «The deployment calculation assumes a linear share of deployment every year from 2022 for each domain in order to reach 100 percent of required deployment by 2050.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* the 13.5 percent and the \"less than 15 percent\" figures describe different measures by the same publisher: the first averages seven domains of the energy system at the end of 2024, the second counts deployment of low-emissions technologies; neither is a measurement of freight.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\n*Analytical inference:* the mechanism that matters to a forwarder is that low-carbon fuels and equipment arrive on a schedule set by cost and by mandate, which sets how long conventional fuel and equipment prices keep driving freight cost. The Global Energy Perspective says why: «The current emphasis on affordability means that some alternative sources, such as green hydrogen and some other sustainable fuels, may not be competitive with traditional fuels in the near term.» It also says «Demand will likely plateau between 2030 and 2035 in the Continued Momentum scenario.» for fossil fuels.\n\nOn progress by domain the Global Institute article says «Momentum is strongest in low-emissions power, electrifying transportation, and critical mineral supplies. Progress is mostly stuck in carbon capture, hydrogen fuels, and heavy industry.» On geography it says «China accounts for about two-thirds of additional solar and wind power and electric vehicle sales since 2022.»\n\nOn trade, the tracking article links tariffs to the transition. It says «fewer resources may be available for decarbonization on a national level, while tariffs could lead to inefficient global supply chains, potentially slowing efforts to transition to cleaner energy.» On aviation it says «Aviation also saw a sharp rise of about 5.5 percent, although its overall share of emissions remains relatively small.»\n\nOn the automotive sector, the tracking article reports that automakers «previously set ambitious targets for electric vehicle (EV) sales by 2030, now plan to continue offering internal combustion engine (ICE) and hybrid vehicles well into the 2030s, if demand persists».\n\n*Operational implication:* a forwarder can read these as a planning signal that fossil fuel stays in the cost base of road, sea and air freight into the 2030s, and that low-carbon fuel premiums are likely to depend on rules rather than on market pull until the 2040s the Perspective names. For clients shipping vehicles, the report that two manufacturers keep combustion and hybrid models well into the 2030s bears on the mix of vehicle types moving. The effect by cargo vertical (live events, artwork, luxury goods, film and television, humanitarian) is not stated by these sources, and the sources state no freight rate or surcharge.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\nWhat it changes is the planning assumption about how fast low-carbon options become available. The Global Energy Perspective states the economic condition: «However, without affordability—along with bankability—widespread adoption of new low-carbon technologies will not happen.»\n\nFor fuel and equipment procurement the decision-relevant result is the 2040 statement quoted in section 1, that alternative fuels are not likely to be broadly adopted before 2040 unless mandated. For commitments, the tracking article reports that others are retreating: «Other countries are scaling back or pushing out their targets.» and gives an example, «the Dutch government recently announced a reduction in its offshore wind commitments, lowering its 2040 target from 50 gigawatts (GW) to between 30 and 40 GW because of rising costs and lower-than-expected electricity demand from industry.»\n\n*Analytical inference:* resting on those statements, a forwarder can say it plans on conventional fuel remaining in its cost base for the near term and treats low-carbon fuel supply as dependent on mandates; it cannot say the transition is on track, because both the tracking article and the Global Institute article say it is not. A claim that a particular fuel will be available or cheap by a given date is not supported by these sources.\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nWhat is not resolved is the path any one region or fuel will follow. The Global Energy Perspective says «Countries and regions will follow distinct trajectories based on local economic conditions, resource endowment, and the realities facing particular industries.» The tracking article states a limit on its targets: «These official and estimated targets do not conform to a singular emissions pathway (for example, 1.5°C or 2°C); however, they are indicative of the trajectory that is required to catalyze the energy transition.»\n\nOn how settled the work is, the Global Institute article describes its figure as an estimate, «We find that an estimated 13.5 percent of the deployment to meet Paris-aligned goals has now been done.», and the held pages state no peer review or independent confirmation. *Analytical inference:* the three publications share one publisher, so their agreement on the 15 percent order of magnitude is the publisher's own, not a second source's.\n\nOn who issued it, the Global Institute page gives «McKinsey Global Institute The hard stuff 2025: Taking stock of progress on the physical challenges of the energy transition November 6, 2025 | Article».\n\n- October 13, 2025: McKinsey Global Energy Perspective 2025 published, in the page's words «October 13, 2025 | Report» (past)\n- November 6, 2025: The hard stuff 2025 published, in the page's words «November 6, 2025 | Article» (past)\n- January 14, 2026: tracking article published, in the page's words «January 14, 2026 | Article» (past)\n- 2030: the year by which two automakers had set electric vehicle sales targets, in the article's words «previously set ambitious targets for electric vehicle (EV) sales by 2030», and the article says they now plan to keep offering combustion engine and hybrid vehicles well into the 2030s if demand persists\n- 2050: the reference year for the Paris-aligned deployment measure, in the article's words «required to meet Paris-aligned targets by 2050», a target and not a deadline\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- mckinsey.com: https://mckinsey.com/capabilities/sustainability/our-insights\n- www.mckinsey.com: https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/energy-and-materials/our-insights/global-energy-perspective\n- www.mckinsey.com: https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/energy-and-materials/our-insights/tracking-the-energy-transition-where-are-we-now\n- www.mckinsey.com: https://www.mckinsey.com/mgi/our-research/the-hard-stuff-2025-taking-stock-of-progress-on-the-physical-challenges-of-the-energy-transition\n",
