   "body": "## 1. What the Research Found\n\nThe research is a study from the International Transport Forum (ITF), the OECD's transport body, on how digital tools can cut freight emissions. Its headline: «Digitalisation will be the core driver of a sustainable recovery of freight transport following the Covid-19 pandemic.» The study «identifies operational improvements digitalisation enables in the sector.»\n\nIts method is modelling: «It quantifies the impact of individual measures on freight CO2 emissions based on the ITF’s global freight model and presents a comprehensive Digital Transformation scenario that demonstrates what the sector can achieve if it applies all levers in a concerted way.» The page holds no figure for any measure, so the size of the saving is not stated here.\n\nTwo OECD publication pages in the same group, «Automated and Autonomous Driving | OECD» and «Preparing Infrastructure for Automated Vehicles | OECD», held only their titles in readable form, so their findings are not stated.\n\n## 2. Why This Finding Matters Operationally and Commercially\n\nFor a forwarder, the study’s subject is the operational side of freight emissions: it asks which operational improvements digitalisation enables and what each is worth in CO2. Its funder links it to data exchange: «This study was funded by the Coalition for Reimagined Mobility (ReMo) and used to develop the findings in their White Paper Solving the Global Supply Chain Crisis with Data Sharing». *Analytical inference:* the page does not name any specific measure, mode, cost or saving, so a forwarder can use the study only to show that such a quantified scenario exists, and no commercial effect is claimed.\n\n## 3. What the Finding Changes for Strategy, Claims, or Decisions\n\nThe decision relevance is limited to claims. The ITF’s page says the work «presents a comprehensive Digital Transformation scenario that demonstrates what the sector can achieve if it applies all levers in a concerted way». A forwarder can say that the ITF, using its global freight model, has examined digital operational measures for freight emissions. It cannot say, from this page, how many tonnes or what share of emissions the measures save, because none is given. The ITF labels the work «Publication Type Case-Specific Policy Analysis Publisher OECD/ITF».\n\n## 5. What the Finding Does Not Resolve\n\nThe methodology limits are those of a scenario: the study «quantifies the impact of individual measures», but the figures and their assumptions are not on the page. The sponsor is named, which a reader should weigh: «This study was funded by the Coalition for Reimagined Mobility (ReMo)». The evidence agreement signal is absent: the page says nothing about peer review or confirmation by other studies. The source authority signal is the publisher line above, OECD/ITF, with «Date of Publication 28 June 2022 Number of Pages 27».\n\nThe two OECD publication pages named in section 1 are not linked to the ITF finding by anything the pages held state, and no finding from them is available in readable form.\n\n- 28 June 2022: ITF study on digitally-driven operational improvements published, «Date of Publication 28 June 2022»\n\n## 6. Sources\n\n- itf-oecd.org: https://www.itf-oecd.org/digitally-driven-operational-improvements-freight-emissions-reduction\n- unece.org: https://unece.org/transport/road-transport/working-party-automatedautonomous-and-connected-vehicles-introduction\n- oecd-ilibrary.org: https://oecd-ilibrary.org/transport/automated-and-autonomous-driving_5jlwvzdfk640-en\n- oecd.org, Automated and Autonomous Driving: https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/automated-and-autonomous-driving_5jlwvzdfk640-en.html\n- oecd.org, Preparing Infrastructure for Automated Vehicles: https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/preparing-infrastructure-for-automated-vehicles_b96ca198-en.html",
