   "body": "## 1. What's Being Tested or Deployed and By Whom\n\nThe activity in the sources is about ammonia as a ship fuel, with hydrogen named alongside it but described far less. Deployed or ordered: the Ammonia Energy Association (AEA), an industry body for ammonia energy, reported in an article «on March 21, 2025» that «WinGD has about 30 orders of its X-DF-A ammonia-powered engines which are set to be delivered for the first time in June.» That article is dated before the delivery month it names, and the sources do not report whether the deliveries took place. The same article says «This year also marks an important milestone with the commercial arrival of the first two-stroke engines.» A second AEA article, published «on January 27, 2025», reports «There are already demonstration vessels on the water using ammonia as a fuel, including small-scale supply vessels that began operations in 2023 and 2024.» Engine testing is named by maker: «Testing results from a number of engine developers (including Wärtsilä , MAN ES , WinGD , and IHI ) indicates that ammonia-fueled engine platforms will produce NO X emissions comparable to engines fueled by heavy maritime oil». On regulation, BIMCO, the shipowners' association, reports «The IMO has issued the Interim guidelines for the safety of ships using ammonia as fuel (MSC.1/Circ.1687), marking a significant milestone in regulatory support for alternative fuels.» and that «The Maritime Safety Committee (MSC) approved the guidelines at its 109th session in December 2024». Hydrogen: the International Maritime Organization (IMO) future fuels site lists a research report titled «SAFE INTRODUCTION OF ALTERNATIVE FUELS (Focus on ammonia and hydrogen as ship fuels)», and another whose stated aim is: «To study the feasibility of hydrogen and ammonia as marine fuels, this research analyses their costs and benefits.» The site describes itself as «Future Fuels and Technology Project is a partnership project between the Government of the Republic of Korea and IMO, aiming to support GHG emissions reduction from international shipping by promoting the uptake of future fuels and technology.» An article is titled «Sustainable Maritime Decarbonization: A Review of Hydrogen and Ammonia as Future Clean Marine Energies», but the page text held gives no findings from it. The sources name no hydrogen-fuelled ship, no hydrogen operator and no hydrogen result. *Analytical inference:* the only quantified deployment is ammonia, in small supply vessels and in engine orders, so a forwarder reading this item should treat hydrogen as a research topic and ammonia as the fuel with ships and engines behind it.\n\n## 2. What This Tells Us About Industry Trajectory\n\nWhy shipping is moving to these fuels: the AEA writes that «By setting absolute targets (20% below 2008 baseline levels in 2030, 70% in 2040 and net-zero in 2050), alternative fuels like ammonia are now necessary for ships to meet their regulatory requirements.» The IMO's stated uptake target, as the AEA quotes it, is «uptake of zero or near-zero GHG emission technologies, fuels and/or energy sources to represent at least 5%, striving for 10%, of the energy used by international shipping by 2030.» Regulation is moving in steps rather than in one decision: the AEA reports that «support for a GHG levy continues to increase» at the IMO, that «support for a goal-based global fuel standard, mirroring the EU’s FuelEU Maritime rules» and that «most European countries back the adoption of a FuelEU Maritime -type scheme at the international level»; it adds that «A “tank-to-wake” methodology is currently used in shipping, meaning fuel production emissions are totally ignored.» The pace on the technical side is set by revision: «The interim guidelines are expected to undergo revisions during CCC 12, scheduled for 2026 or 2027.» *Analytical inference:* the drivers in the sources are regulation and the IMO's own targets, with engine makers and ship builders following; the sources show several engine makers testing and orders placed, which points to a multi-supplier effort rather than a single operator's experiment, but they name no client demand signal for ammonia-fuelled transport.\n\n## 3. Supplier Access and Procurement Reality\n\nWho can build and supply: the AEA article names four engine developers in testing and one with orders, «WinGD has about 30 orders of its X-DF-A ammonia-powered engines which are set to be delivered for the first time in June.» It also names two companies with ammonia gas carriers under construction: «opening the door for ammonia-fueled, ammonia gas carriers to operate, such as those under construction by NYK Line and EXMAR». Fuel supply is the open link. The AEA writes of FuelEU Maritime pooling that «A few alternatively-fueled vessels may thus be able to achieve the FuelEU Maritime goals of an entire fleet, encouraging shipowners to deploy these new technologies, secure a certified bunker fuel supply, and operate» if only in a limited way to begin with. The AEA adds that «Establishing consistent global regulatory standards, certification frameworks, and product specifications is also essential to support widespread adoption» *Analytical inference:* the orders reported and the demonstration vessels show ammonia engines in use or on order, while the sources leave open who sells ammonia fuel to a ship and at what price. Gap: the sources name no price, delivery lead time, financing structure or exclusive arrangement for ammonia or hydrogen ships or fuel, and nothing is stated on who a non-owner, such as a cargo shipper or a forwarder, can buy this from.\n\n## 4. Operational Fit by Transport Mode and Cargo Vertical\n\nOcean is the only mode the sources reach. The BIMCO report describes the safety framework as «covering ship design, equipment, operations, bunkering, toxicity mitigation, and crew protection», and says «While the guidelines are non-mandatory, they align closely with the IGF Code and SOLAS principles». The AEA lists provisions of the interim guidelines, which BIMCO calls non-mandatory: «Storage of ammonia fuel in a refrigerated state at atmospheric pressure.» and «Alarm thresholds are set at 25 ppm for localized alerts, 110 ppm for global alerts, and 220 ppm for system shutdowns.» On cargo, the AEA notes that «Under the current IGC Code, the use of toxic gas cargo for fuel (i.e. ammonia) is prohibited.» with draft guidelines that the AEA expected to be finalized at «Draft interim guidelines for using ammonia as cargo and fuel under the IGC Code are expected to be finalized at CCC 11 in September 2025», a date now past whose outcome the sources do not report, and that «But the IGF Code does not cover low-flashpoint oil, methanol, ethanol, LPG, ammonia or hydrogen fuels.» *Analytical inference:* a ship burning ammonia carries a toxic fuel, so bunkering practice and crew training are among the topics the guidelines cover; the BIMCO report adds that fuel-specific crew training is being developed in the order «training programs for methyl/ethyl alcohol, ammonia, fuel cells, LPG, hydrogen, and batteries to be developed in that order». The sources give no verdict for road or air, no cargo vertical and no route, so how ammonia or hydrogen fuel fits any lane or cargo type is not stated. *Legal Confirmation Required:* whether and from when the FuelEU Maritime limits apply to a given vessel or port call, a question for counsel. The FuelEU Maritime link matters for European lanes: the AEA writes that «FuelEU Maritime targets all vessels above 5,000 gross tonnage calling at European ports, mandating GHG reductions in energy used on board. Beginning with a 2% reduction this year, by 2050 all such vessels will need to achieve an 80% reduction.» (the article is dated March 21, 2025, so its \"this year\" is 2025) and the same article adds that most European countries back such a scheme at the IMO.\n\n## 6. Conversational and Strategic Talking Points\n\n- Say that the IMO's non-binding framework exists for ammonia ships: BIMCO describes it as «While the guidelines are non-mandatory, they align closely with the IGF Code and SOLAS principles», published on «Published 27 March 2025».\n- Ask a carrier which fuel its newbuild order uses and whether it is entered in a FuelEU Maritime pool, since the AEA says pooling lets a few alternatively-fuelled vessels meet the goals of an entire fleet.\n- Avoid presenting hydrogen as in service: the sources hold reports and a review, and no hydrogen vessel.\n\n## 7. Time-to-Market, Procurement Window, and Action\n\nMONITORING: the sources give no date by which ammonia or hydrogen ships are available to charter at scale; the dated points they hold are that «The interim guidelines are expected to undergo revisions during CCC 12, scheduled for 2026 or 2027.» and the IMO's 2030 uptake target.\n\n- A trade-lane manager for European liner and charter bookings, at the next ocean carrier contract renewal, adds a question asking each carrier to name its alternative fuel newbuilds, engine maker and delivery year, and files the answers beside the FuelEU Maritime figures quoted above. *Operational implication:* this gives a dated list of which carrier fleets could carry ammonia-fuelled tonnage.\n- A procurement lead sends the carrier panel the IMO interim guideline reference MSC.1/Circ.1687 and asks which of the ships on offer are designed to it, before the CCC 12 revision in 2026 or 2027 changes the text.\n- A sustainability lead records that no source gives a hydrogen vessel result and holds hydrogen out of client claims until one is sourced.\n\n- December 2024: the Maritime Safety Committee approves the interim guidelines for ammonia as fuel, per BIMCO.\n- 27 March 2025: BIMCO publishes its note on the IMO interim guidelines.\n- 2026 or 2027: CCC 12 scheduled to revise the interim guidelines.\n- 2030: the IMO uptake target for zero or near-zero GHG emission fuels is stated to apply.\n\n## 8. Sources\n\n- International Maritime Organization, Future Fuels and Technology, hydrogen archive: https://futurefuels.imo.org/fuel-type/hydrogen\n- BIMCO, IMO issues interim guidelines for safety of ammonia-fuelled ships, 27 March 2025: https://www.bimco.org/news-insights/bimco-news/2025/03/27-ammonia/\n- Ammonia Energy Association, Setting the scene for ammonia maritime fuel: https://ammoniaenergy.org/articles/setting-the-scene-for-ammonia-maritime-fuel-regulatory-needs-and-timelines-to-decarbonize-shipping/\n- Ammonia Energy Association, Paving the way for ammonia as a marine fuel: https://ammoniaenergy.org/articles/paving-the-way-for-ammonia-as-a-marine-fuel-insights-into-the-imos-interim-guidelines/\n- MDPI Sustainability, Sustainable Maritime Decarbonization review: https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/17/24/11364\n",
